window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-chem-atom","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Structure of Atom · Practice","chapter":"Structure of Atom","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"When a narrow beam of white light passes through a glass prism, it spreads into coloured bands. Which colour is deviated the least from its original path, and why?","image":"","options":["Violet, because it has the shortest wavelength","Red, because it has the longest wavelength","Green, because it lies in the middle of the visible range","Violet, because it has the lowest frequency"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a prism, light of shorter wavelength bends more than light of longer wavelength.\nRed has the longest wavelength in the visible range, so it is deviated the least.\nViolet has the shortest wavelength, so it is deviated the most. It also has the highest frequency, not the lowest, so option (4) is wrong on both counts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"The threshold frequency of potassium is $5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. What is the work function of potassium? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s)","image":"","options":["$3.313 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$3.313 \\times 10^{-18}$ J","$3.313 \\times 10^{-20}$ J","$1.325 \\times 10^{-19}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu_0 = 5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz and $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s.\nFormula: $W_0 = h\\nu_0$.\nSubstituting: $W_0 = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ J.\nSo $W_0 = 3.313 \\times 10^{-19}$ J, which is about 2.07 eV.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity in Bohr's model for a hydrogen-like species) with List-II (proportional to / given by).\nList-I:\n(a) Radius of stationary state\n(b) Energy of stationary state\n(c) Angular momentum of electron\n(d) Frequency of radiation in a transition\nList-II:\n(i) n\n(ii) $\\frac{n^2}{Z}$\n(iii) $-\\frac{Z^2}{n^2}$\n(iv) $\\frac{\\Delta E}{h}$","image":"","options":["a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv","a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $r_n = \\frac{52.9\\, n^2}{Z}$ pm, so a matches (ii).\n(b) $E_n = -2.18 \\times 10^{-18} \\frac{Z^2}{n^2}$ J, so b matches (iii).\n(c) $m_e v r = \\frac{nh}{2\\pi}$, so angular momentum is proportional to n; c matches (i).\n(d) Bohr's frequency rule gives $\\nu = \\frac{\\Delta E}{h}$, so d matches (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A photon of violet light carries more energy than a photon of red light.\nReason (R): The energy of a photon is directly proportional to its wavelength.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Violet light (about 400 nm, $7.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz) has a higher frequency than red light (about 750 nm, $4.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz). Since $E = h\\nu$, the violet photon carries more energy.\nR is false. From $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$, photon energy is inversely proportional to wavelength, not directly proportional.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Work functions of some metals (in eV) are: Li 2.42, Na 2.3, K 2.25, Mg 3.7, Cu 4.8, Ag 4.3. Monochromatic ultraviolet light of wavelength 300 nm falls on each metal in turn. From how many of these metals will photoelectrons be emitted? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$, 1 eV $= 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ J)","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 300$ nm.\nPhoton energy: $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda} = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{300 \\times 10^{-9}} = 6.626 \\times 10^{-19}$ J. Dividing by $1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ gives $E \\approx 4.14$ eV.\nCondition for emission: $h\\nu > W_0$.\nLi (2.42), Na (2.3), K (2.25) and Mg (3.7) all have $W_0$ below 4.14 eV, so they emit.\nAg (4.3) and Cu (4.8) have $W_0$ above 4.14 eV, so they do not emit. Ag is the tempting trap, since its value is only slightly higher.\nSo 4 metals emit photoelectrons.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which electronic transition in the $He^+$ ion produces a photon of the same wavelength as the first line of the Lyman series of the hydrogen atom?","image":"","options":["$n = 4 \\to n = 2$","$n = 2 \\to n = 1$","$n = 3 \\to n = 2$","$n = 6 \\to n = 4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For a hydrogen-like species, $\\Delta E \\propto Z^2\\left(\\frac{1}{n_f^2} - \\frac{1}{n_i^2}\\right)$.\nFirst Lyman line of H ($2 \\to 1$, $Z = 1$): $1 \\times (1 - \\frac{1}{4}) = 0.75$.\nCheck each $He^+$ option ($Z^2 = 4$):\n$4 \\to 2$: $4(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{16}) = 0.75$, which matches.\n$2 \\to 1$: $4 \\times 0.75 = 3$. $3 \\to 2$: $4 \\times \\frac{5}{36} = 0.556$. $6 \\to 4$: $4 \\times \\frac{20}{576} = 0.139$.\nEqual energy means equal wavelength.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In Thomson's apparatus, electric and magnetic fields act perpendicular to each other and to the electron beam.\nWith only the electric field on, the beam strikes the screen at A.\nWith only the magnetic field on, it strikes at C.\nWith both fields suitably balanced, it strikes at B, the undeflected position.\nWith only the electric field on, which change would move the spot further away from B?","image":"","options":["Replacing the electrons by heavier particles carrying the same negative charge","Changing the cathode from aluminium to copper","Increasing the voltage applied across the electric field plates","Reducing the strength of the electric field"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that deflection increases with larger charge, smaller mass and greater field strength (or voltage).\n(3) Correct: a higher voltage means a stronger field, so the spot at A moves further from B.\n(1) Wrong: heavier particles with the same charge are deflected less.\n(2) Wrong: cathode ray properties do not depend on the electrode material.\n(4) Wrong: a weaker field reduces the deflection.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An electron and a proton have the same kinetic energy. Taking $m_e = 9.109 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg and $m_p = 1.6726 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg, the ratio of the de Broglie wavelength of the electron to that of the proton, $\\frac{\\lambda_e}{\\lambda_p}$, is approximately:","image":"","options":["42.9","1836","0.0233","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: equal kinetic energy K for both particles.\nFormula: $p = \\sqrt{2mK}$, so $\\lambda = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2mK}}$.\nTherefore $\\frac{\\lambda_e}{\\lambda_p} = \\sqrt{\\frac{m_p}{m_e}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\sqrt{\\frac{1.6726 \\times 10^{-27}}{9.109 \\times 10^{-31}}} = \\sqrt{1836} \\approx 42.9$.\nThe value 1836 would be the ratio only at equal velocities, not equal kinetic energies.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following sets of quantum numbers $(n, l, m_l, m_s)$ is NOT permitted for an electron in an atom?","image":"","options":["$(3, 2, -2, +\\frac{1}{2})$","$(2, 1, 0, -\\frac{1}{2})$","$(3, 3, +1, +\\frac{1}{2})$","$(4, 0, 0, -\\frac{1}{2})$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The rules are: l can be 0 to $n-1$; $m_l$ can be $-l$ to $+l$; $m_s = \\pm\\frac{1}{2}$.\n(1) Allowed: this is a 3d electron.\n(2) Allowed: this is a 2p electron.\n(3) Not allowed: for $n = 3$ the largest l is 2, so $l = 3$ is impossible.\n(4) Allowed: this is a 4s electron.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In an oil drop experiment, three droplets carry charges of $4.806 \\times 10^{-19}$ C, $8.010 \\times 10^{-19}$ C and $11.214 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. Assuming every droplet charge is an integral multiple of one fundamental charge, what is the LARGEST value of that fundamental charge consistent with these data?","image":"","options":["$1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ C","$3.204 \\times 10^{-19}$ C","$0.801 \\times 10^{-19}$ C","$4.806 \\times 10^{-19}$ C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the three charges are 4.806, 8.010 and $11.214 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nIdea: each charge equals ne, so e must divide all three exactly. The largest such e is their greatest common divisor.\nDividing each by $1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ C gives 3, 5 and 7. These have no common factor, so no larger unit works.\nA value of $3.204 \\times 10^{-19}$ C would give 1.5, 2.5 and 3.5, which are not integers.\nThe value $0.801 \\times 10^{-19}$ C also divides all three, but it is smaller.\nSo the largest fundamental charge is $1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a multi-electron atom, the 2s orbital is lower in energy than the 2p orbital.\nReason (R): A 2s electron penetrates closer to the nucleus than a 2p electron, so it experiences a greater effective nuclear charge.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in multi-electron atoms the energy depends on both $n$ and $l$. For the same $n$, the order is s < p < d < f.\nR is true: an s electron spends more time near the nucleus than a p electron of the same shell. It is therefore shielded less by the inner electrons.\nThe greater effective nuclear charge binds the 2s electron more tightly and lowers its energy. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"For the 3d electrons only, compare the number of possible exchanges between electrons of parallel spin in the actual configuration of chromium ($3d^5 4s^1$) with that in the hypothetical configuration $3d^4 4s^2$. The respective numbers are:","image":"","options":["10 and 6","5 and 4","10 and 4","20 and 12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An exchange is a swap of positions between any two electrons of the same spin in degenerate orbitals.\nThe number of exchanges equals the number of pairs, $\\frac{N(N-1)}{2}$, where $N$ is the number of parallel-spin electrons.\nFor $3d^5$: $\\frac{5 \\times 4}{2} = 10$.\nFor $3d^4$: $\\frac{4 \\times 3}{2} = 6$.\nMore exchanges mean a larger exchange energy, which helps explain why Cr prefers $3d^5 4s^1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about cathode rays are correct?\n(i) They travel from the cathode towards the anode.\n(ii) The rays themselves are visible as a glowing beam inside the tube.\n(iii) In the absence of electric and magnetic fields, they travel in straight lines.\n(iv) Their characteristics depend on the nature of the gas filled in the tube.\n(v) Their deflection increases as the strength of the applied magnetic field increases.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: cathode rays start at the cathode and move towards the anode.\n(ii) Incorrect: the rays are invisible. They are detected only by the glow of fluorescent materials they strike.\n(iii) Correct: with no fields applied, they travel in straight lines.\n(iv) Incorrect: their characteristics are independent of the gas and of the electrode material.\n(v) Correct: deflection grows with field strength.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Assuming the electrons to be stationary does not rescue Rutherford's model, because electrostatic attraction would pull them towards the nucleus and give a miniature version of Thomson's atom.\nStatement II: Planets revolving round the sun also lose energy steadily by emitting electromagnetic radiation, because they too are accelerated.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT notes that stationary electrons would be pulled towards the dense nucleus by electrostatic attraction. This would produce a miniature Thomson-type atom, so stationary electrons do not solve the problem.\nStatement II is incorrect. Planets are uncharged, so the radiation argument does not apply to them, even though they are accelerated.\nThat difference is exactly why the solar-system analogy fails for electrons. An orbiting electron would spiral into the nucleus in about $10^{-8}$ s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Using the Rydberg constant for hydrogen, $R_H = 109677\\ cm^{-1}$, calculate the wavenumber of the first (lowest-energy) line of the Lyman series of hydrogen.","image":"","options":["82258 $cm^{-1}$","15233 $cm^{-1}$","109677 $cm^{-1}$","97491 $cm^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Lyman series, so $n_1 = 1$. The first line corresponds to $n_2 = 2$. $R_H = 109677\\ cm^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\bar{\\nu} = R_H\\left(\\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \\frac{1}{n_2^2}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $\\bar{\\nu} = 109677 \\times \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{4}\\right) = 109677 \\times \\frac{3}{4}$\nSo $\\bar{\\nu} \\approx 82258\\ cm^{-1}$.\nFor comparison, 97491 $cm^{-1}$ is the second Lyman line ($n_2 = 3$), 15233 $cm^{-1}$ is the first Balmer line, and 109677 $cm^{-1}$ is the Lyman series limit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (series in the hydrogen spectrum) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\n(A) Lyman\n(B) Balmer\n(C) Brackett\n(D) Pfund\nList-II:\n(I) Lines end at $n = 4$\n(II) Lies in the ultraviolet region\n(III) Upper level $n_2$ starts from 6\n(IV) Only series in the visible region","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Lyman has $n_1 = 1$ and lies in the ultraviolet region, so A-II.\n(B) Balmer has $n_1 = 2$ and is the only series in the visible region, so B-IV.\n(C) Brackett has $n_1 = 4$, so its lines end at $n = 4$: C-I.\n(D) Pfund has $n_1 = 5$, so $n_2 = 6, 7, \\ldots$: D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a limitation of Bohr's model of the atom?","image":"","options":["It cannot explain the splitting of spectral lines in a magnetic field (Zeeman effect)","It cannot explain the doublets seen in the hydrogen spectrum with sophisticated instruments","It cannot account for the line spectrum of hydrogen-like ions such as $He^+$ and $Li^{2+}$","It cannot explain how atoms combine to form molecules through chemical bonds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Bohr's model does account for the stability and line spectra of hydrogen and hydrogen-like ions such as $He^+$, $Li^{2+}$ and $Be^{3+}$. So option (3) is not a limitation.\nThe model's actual failures are:\n(a) it misses the fine details (doublets) of the hydrogen spectrum;\n(b) it cannot explain the Zeeman and Stark effects;\n(c) it cannot explain spectra of multi-electron atoms such as helium;\n(d) it cannot explain chemical bonding.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"According to Planck's quantum theory, a collection of identical oscillators emitting radiation of frequency $\\nu$ can lose energy only in certain amounts. Which of the following amounts of energy can NOT be emitted?","image":"","options":["$h\\nu$","$3h\\nu$","$2.5h\\nu$","$5h\\nu$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: by Planck, energy is emitted or absorbed only in whole quanta. The allowed values are $E = 0, h\\nu, 2h\\nu, 3h\\nu, \\ldots, nh\\nu$, where $n$ is an integer.\n$h\\nu$, $3h\\nu$ and $5h\\nu$ are integral multiples of $h\\nu$, so they are allowed.\n$2.5h\\nu$ would need half a quantum. This is like standing between two steps of a staircase, so it is not allowed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The charge to mass ratio of the particles in canal rays changes when the gas in the discharge tube is changed.\nReason (R): Canal ray particles are positively charged ions of the gas present in the tube.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the e/m of the positive particles depends on the gas from which they originate. This is unlike cathode rays, whose properties stay the same for every gas.\nR is true. Canal rays are simply positively charged gaseous ions.\nDifferent gases give ions of different masses, and some ions carry multiple charges. So e/m changes with the gas, and R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about X-rays is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["They are produced effectively when electrons strike a dense metal anode (target)","They have very short wavelengths, of the order of 0.1 nm","They are deflected towards the positive plate in an electric field","Their high penetrating power makes them useful for studying the interior of objects"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: X-rays form effectively when electrons hit dense metal targets.\n(2) Correct: their wavelength is about 0.1 nm, and they are electromagnetic in nature.\n(3) Incorrect: X-rays carry no charge, so electric and magnetic fields do not deflect them. Being drawn to the positive plate is the behaviour of negatively charged particles such as cathode rays.\n(4) Correct: their high penetration is used to view the interior of objects.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An electron in the first Bohr orbit of $He^+$ moves faster than an electron in the first Bohr orbit of a hydrogen atom.\nReason (R): In Bohr's model, the speed of the electron increases with the positive charge on the nucleus and decreases as the principal quantum number increases.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. NCERT states that the electron's speed increases with nuclear charge and decreases with n.\nA is true. Both electrons are in n = 1, but $He^+$ has Z = 2 while H has Z = 1. The higher nuclear charge makes the $He^+$ electron faster.\nSince the only difference between the two cases is Z, R directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Radiation of wavelength 400 nm falls on potassium (work function 2.25 eV). What is the de Broglie wavelength of the fastest photoelectron emitted? (h = $6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, c = $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$, 1 eV = $1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ J, $m_e$ = $9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg)","image":"","options":["1.33 nm","0.94 nm","2.66 nm","0.67 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda$ = 400 nm, $W_0$ = 2.25 eV = $3.60 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nFormula: KE = $\\frac{hc}{\\lambda} - W_0$, $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2KE}{m}}$, $\\lambda_e = \\frac{h}{mv}$.\nSubstituting: photon energy = $4.97 \\times 10^{-19}$ J, so KE = $1.37 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nThen $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 1.37 \\times 10^{-19}}{9.1 \\times 10^{-31}}} \\approx 5.48 \\times 10^{5}$ m s$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\lambda_e = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 5.48 \\times 10^{5}} \\approx 1.33 \\times 10^{-9}$ m = 1.33 nm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In Thomson's plum-pudding model of the atom, which of the following is an assumed feature?","image":"","options":["The mass of the atom is uniformly distributed over the whole atom","The positive charge is concentrated in a tiny central nucleus","Electrons revolve around the positive charge in fixed circular orbits","The radius of the atom is about $10^{-15}$ m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Thomson pictured the atom as a sphere of radius about $10^{-10}$ m. Its positive charge was spread uniformly, with electrons embedded in it like seeds in a watermelon.\nNCERT stresses one key feature of this model: the mass of the atom is spread uniformly over the atom.\nA concentrated nucleus and electrons in orbits belong to Rutherford's model, not Thomson's.\n$10^{-15}$ m is the size of a nucleus, not of an atom.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"For hydrogen-like species, take $r_n = \\frac{52.9\\,n^2}{Z}$ pm and $E_n = -2.18 \\times 10^{-18}\\frac{Z^2}{n^2}$ J. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The radius of the $n = 2$ orbit of He$^+$ is twice the radius of the first Bohr orbit of H.\n(ii) The energy of the $n = 3$ state of Li$^{2+}$ equals the ground state energy of H.\n(iii) The energy of the $n = 2$ state of He$^+$ equals the ground state energy of H.\n(iv) The radius of the $n = 3$ orbit of Li$^{2+}$ equals the radius of the first Bohr orbit of H.\n(v) The ionisation energy of ground state He$^+$ is twice that of ground state H.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. $r = \\frac{52.9 \\times 4}{2} = 105.8$ pm, which is $2 \\times 52.9$ pm.\n(ii) Correct. $\\frac{Z^2}{n^2} = \\frac{9}{9} = 1$, the same as H with $n = 1$.\n(iii) Correct. $\\frac{Z^2}{n^2} = \\frac{4}{4} = 1$.\n(iv) Incorrect. $r = \\frac{52.9 \\times 9}{3} = 158.7$ pm, which is $3a_0$.\n(v) Incorrect. Energy scales as $Z^2$, so the ionisation energy is 4 times that of H.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Using Bohr's theory for hydrogen-like species, $E_n = -2.18\\times10^{-18}\\left(\\frac{Z^2}{n^2}\\right)$ J. What is the energy of the electron in the $n = 3$ orbit of $Li^{2+}$ ($Z = 3$)?","image":"","options":["$-2.18\\times10^{-18}$ J","$-6.54\\times10^{-18}$ J","$-0.727\\times10^{-18}$ J","$-19.62\\times10^{-18}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $Z = 3$, $n = 3$.\nFormula: $E_n = -2.18\\times10^{-18}\\,Z^2/n^2$ J.\nSubstituting: $E_3 = -2.18\\times10^{-18}\\times 9/9$.\nSo $E_3 = -2.18\\times10^{-18}$ J, the same as the ground-state energy of the H atom.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II (total number of orbitals).\nList-I:\n(P) Shell with n = 4\n(Q) Subshell with l = 3\n(R) Shell with n = 3\n(S) Subshell with n = 2, l = 1\nList-II:\n(i) 3\n(ii) 9\n(iii) 7\n(iv) 16","image":"","options":["P-ii, Q-iii, R-iv, S-i","P-iv, Q-iii, R-ii, S-i","P-iv, Q-i, R-ii, S-iii","P-iii, Q-iv, R-ii, S-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A shell has $n^2$ orbitals, so n = 4 gives 16 (P-iv) and n = 3 gives 9 (R-ii).\nA subshell has $2l+1$ orbitals, so l = 3 gives 7 (Q-iii).\nThe subshell n = 2, l = 1 is 2p, with $2(1)+1 = 3$ orbitals (S-i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In Thomson's model, the mass of the atom is assumed to be uniformly distributed over the whole atom.\n(ii) In Rutherford's gold foil experiment, roughly 1 in 20,000 $\\alpha$-particles was deflected by nearly $180^\\circ$.\n(iii) $\\gamma$-rays are a stream of neutral particles whose penetrating power is about 1000 times that of $\\alpha$-particles.\n(iv) X-rays are deflected by a magnetic field because they are produced when electrons strike a metal target.\n(v) By Maxwell's electromagnetic theory, an electron revolving in a Rutherford orbit should radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Thomson assumed that both the positive charge and the mass are spread uniformly over the atom.\n(ii) Correct: very few $\\alpha$-particles, about 1 in 20,000, bounced back.\n(iii) Incorrect: $\\gamma$-rays are high-energy radiation like X-rays. They do not consist of particles, though their penetrating power is about 1000 times that of $\\alpha$-particles.\n(iv) Incorrect: X-rays are electromagnetic radiation and are not deflected by electric or magnetic fields.\n(v) Correct: an accelerating charge should emit radiation, so the orbit would shrink in about $10^{-8}$ s. This means Rutherford's model cannot explain why atoms are stable.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Aufbau principle\n(B) Pauli exclusion principle\n(C) Hund's rule of maximum multiplicity\n(D) $(n+l)$ rule\nList-II\n(I) No two electrons in an atom can have the same set of four quantum numbers\n(II) Orbitals are filled in order of increasing energy\n(III) Of two orbitals with equal $(n+l)$, the one with lower n is filled first\n(IV) Pairing in degenerate orbitals begins only after each has one electron","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The aufbau principle says orbitals are filled in increasing order of energy, so A-II.\n(B) The Pauli principle says no two electrons can share all four quantum numbers, so B-I.\n(C) Hund's rule says electrons do not pair in degenerate orbitals until each orbital is singly occupied, so C-IV.\n(D) When two orbitals have the same $(n+l)$, the $(n+l)$ rule fills the one with lower n first, so D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The square of the orbital wave function, $|\\psi|^2$, at a point gives the probability density of the electron at that point and is always positive.\nStatement II: The wave function $\\psi$ itself directly gives the probability of finding the electron at a point.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Following Born's interpretation, $|\\psi|^2$ is the probability density (probability per unit volume). Since it is a square, it is never negative.\nStatement II is incorrect. By itself, ψ has no physical meaning and it can even take negative values. To get a probability, $|\\psi|^2$ must be multiplied by a small volume element.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"According to Bohr's theory, the energy of the electron in a hydrogen atom is negative for every allowed orbit. What does this negative sign mean?","image":"","options":["The electron in the atom has lower energy than a free electron at rest, which is infinitely far from the nucleus and is assigned zero energy","The electron has negative kinetic energy while it moves in its orbit","The electron loses energy continuously as it revolves, so its energy keeps falling below zero","The electron's energy is less than that of the nucleus, which is taken as zero"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The reference state for energy is a free electron at rest, infinitely far from the nucleus ($n = \\infty$, $E_\\infty = 0$).\nA bound electron is attracted by the nucleus, so energy is released when it is bound. Its energy is therefore below zero, which shows how stable it is.\nKinetic energy can never be negative, so option (2) fails.\nIn a stationary state the energy does not change with time, so option (3) contradicts Bohr's postulate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (number of neutrons in one atom).\nList-I:\n(A) Protium\n(B) Deuterium\n(C) Tritium\n(D) $^{37}_{17}\\text{Cl}$\nList-II:\n(I) 2\n(II) 0\n(III) 20\n(IV) 1","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Protium $^{1}_{1}\\text{H}$: 1 − 1 = 0 neutrons, so A-II.\nDeuterium $^{2}_{1}\\text{D}$: 2 − 1 = 1 neutron, so B-IV.\nTritium $^{3}_{1}\\text{T}$: 3 − 1 = 2 neutrons, so C-I.\nCl-37: 37 − 17 = 20 neutrons, so D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Very bright red light of frequency about $4.5\\times10^{14}$ Hz shining on potassium (threshold frequency $5.0\\times10^{14}$ Hz) for hours ejects no photoelectrons.\nStatement II: In the photoelectric effect, the kinetic energy of the ejected electrons increases as the intensity of light increases.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The red light's frequency is below $\\nu_0$, so each photon has too little energy to eject an electron. Brightness only adds more such photons, so no emission occurs.\nStatement II is incorrect. Intensity changes only the number of electrons ejected.\nThe kinetic energy $h(\\nu-\\nu_0)$ depends on frequency, not intensity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A 4d orbital has two radial nodes.\nStatement II: The number of angular nodes in an orbital is equal to its azimuthal quantum number $l$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Number of radial nodes = $n - l - 1$. For 4d ($n = 4$, $l = 2$), this is $4 - 2 - 1 = 1$, not 2. So Statement I is incorrect.\nNumber of angular nodes = l. For 4d this gives 2, and the total number of nodes is $n - 1 = 3$. So Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"When a metal surface is irradiated with light of frequency $8.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons is K. When light of frequency $1.2 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz is used, the maximum kinetic energy becomes 2K. What is the threshold frequency of the metal?","image":"","options":["$6.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$2.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$4.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: KE $= h(\\nu - \\nu_0)$ for each frequency.\nFirst light: $h(8.0 \\times 10^{14} - \\nu_0) = K$.\nSecond light: $h(1.2 \\times 10^{15} - \\nu_0) = 2K$.\nDividing the second equation by the first: $1.2 \\times 10^{15} - \\nu_0 = 2(8.0 \\times 10^{14} - \\nu_0)$.\nThis gives $\\nu_0 = 1.6 \\times 10^{15} - 1.2 \\times 10^{15} = 4.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"The threshold frequency of potassium is $5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. Which of the following will eject photoelectrons from a clean potassium surface?","image":"","options":["Very bright red light ($4.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz) shone for several hours","Very weak yellow light ($5.15 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz)","Highly intense infrared radiation ($10^{13}$ Hz)","High-intensity microwave radiation ($10^{10}$ Hz)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Photoemission occurs only if $\\nu > \\nu_0$. Intensity and exposure time cannot make up for a frequency below threshold.\nOnly yellow light ($5.15 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz) exceeds $5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. So even a weak yellow beam ejects electrons.\nThe tempting option is bright red light. Its frequency is below threshold, so no electrons are emitted however long it shines. Infrared and microwave fail for the same reason.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bohr's model of the hydrogen atom contradicts Heisenberg's uncertainty principle.\nReason (R): A Bohr orbit is a clearly defined path, which can be specified only if the exact position and exact velocity of the electron are known at the same time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the Bohr model ignores the dual behaviour of matter and also contradicts the uncertainty principle.\nR is true. A well-defined orbit requires knowing the electron's position and velocity simultaneously and exactly.\nR explains A: knowing both exactly at once is exactly what Heisenberg's principle forbids.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For light of frequency above the threshold frequency, making the beam brighter increases the number of photoelectrons ejected per second but leaves their kinetic energy unchanged.\nReason (R): A more intense beam of a given frequency contains more photons, but each photon still carries the same energy $h\\nu$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that the number of electrons ejected is proportional to the intensity of light. The kinetic energy of these electrons does not depend on brightness.\nR is true. Intensity reflects the number of photons. The energy of each photon is $h\\nu$, which depends only on frequency.\nR explains A. More photons eject more electrons. Each electron still receives the same energy, $h\\nu - h\\nu_0$, so its kinetic energy is unchanged.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A narrow beam of white light passes through a glass prism and spreads into a band of colours. Which statement correctly describes what happens?","image":"","options":["Violet light, having the longest wavelength, is deviated the most","Red light, having the longest wavelength, is deviated the least","All colours are deviated equally because they travel at the same speed in glass","Red light, having the shortest wavelength, is deviated the most"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Light changes speed when it enters a new medium, so it is refracted, and the amount of bending depends on wavelength.\nShorter wavelengths bend more than longer ones.\nRed has the longest visible wavelength, so it is deviated the least. Violet has the shortest wavelength, so it is deviated the most.\nOption (1) is tempting because violet is indeed deviated most, but it wrongly calls violet the longest wavelength.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An electron (mass $9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg) moves with a velocity of $2.0 \\times 10^{6}$ m s$^{-1}$. What is its de Broglie wavelength? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s)","image":"","options":["0.364 nm","0.182 nm","3.64 nm","0.728 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg, $v = 2.0 \\times 10^{6}$ m s$^{-1}$, $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{h}{mv}$.\nSubstituting: $mv = 1.82 \\times 10^{-24}$ kg m s$^{-1}$, so $\\lambda = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{1.82 \\times 10^{-24}} = 3.64 \\times 10^{-10}$ m.\nSo $\\lambda$ = 0.364 nm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Spectroscopic analysis led to the discovery of several elements. Which element was discovered in the sun, rather than in a mineral on earth, by the spectroscopic method?","image":"","options":["Rubidium","Helium","Caesium","Gallium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, helium was discovered in the sun by the spectroscopic method.\nRubidium, caesium and gallium (along with thallium, indium and scandium) were discovered when their minerals were analysed spectroscopically.\nThe three distractors are therefore elements found through mineral analysis, not through solar spectra.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In a sample containing a large number of hydrogen atoms, the electrons are excited to the $n = 6$ level. As they return to lower levels by all possible transitions, how many of the emitted spectral lines belong to the Balmer series?","image":"","options":["5","15","10","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A Balmer line is any transition that ends at $n_f = 2$.\nThe possible starting levels are $n_i = 3, 4, 5, 6$. These give 4 transitions: 3→2, 4→2, 5→2 and 6→2.\nThe total number of lines from $n = 6$ is $\\frac{6 \\times 5}{2} = 15$, but that count includes the Lyman, Paschen, Brackett and Pfund lines as well.\nThe value 5 is the number of Lyman lines, since 6→1 through 2→1 gives five transitions.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A certain subshell has seven possible values of the magnetic quantum number $m_l$. What are its azimuthal quantum number and its notation?","image":"","options":["$l = 2$, d subshell","$l = 3$, f subshell","$l = 4$, g subshell","$l = 6$, f subshell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A subshell with azimuthal quantum number l has $2l+1$ values of $m_l$.\nSetting $2l + 1 = 7$ gives $l = 3$.\nThe value $l = 3$ is written as f, so the subshell has seven f orbitals.\n$l = 6$ comes from wrongly taking the number of $m_l$ values as $l + 1$. The d subshell ($l = 2$) has only 5 orbitals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"For two metals P and Q, the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons is plotted on the y-axis against the frequency of incident light on the x-axis. Metal Q has the larger work function. Which statement about the graphs is correct?","image":"","options":["Both graphs are straight lines of equal slope $h$, and the line for Q cuts the frequency axis at a higher frequency","The line for Q is steeper because Q has the larger work function","Both graphs pass through the origin with slopes proportional to their work functions","The graphs are curves whose kinetic energy rises with the square of the frequency"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"From $\\frac{1}{2}m_ev^2 = h\\nu - h\\nu_0$, KE is a linear function of $\\nu$.\nThe slope is $h$, which is the same for every metal, so the two lines are parallel.\nKE becomes zero at $\\nu = \\nu_0 = W_0/h$, so the metal with the larger $W_0$ (Q) cuts the frequency axis at a higher frequency.\nThe lines do not pass through the origin, and the slope does not depend on $W_0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An atomic orbital has 2 radial nodes and 1 angular node. Which orbital is it?","image":"","options":["3p","4p","4d","5p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: The number of angular nodes equals l. One angular node means l = 1, so the orbital is a p orbital.\nStep 2: The number of radial nodes equals n − l − 1. Setting n − 1 − 1 = 2 gives n = 4.\nStep 3: The orbital is therefore 4p.\nStep 4: Check the others: 3p has 1 radial node; 4d has 1 radial node and 2 angular nodes; 5p has 3 radial nodes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Monochromatic light of frequency greater than the threshold frequency falls on a clean potassium surface. The frequency is kept the same and the intensity of the light is doubled. What happens?","image":"","options":["The kinetic energy of each ejected electron doubles, but their number stays the same","The number of ejected electrons increases, but their maximum kinetic energy stays the same","Both the number and the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected electrons double","Electrons are ejected after a shorter time lag, but their number stays the same"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A more intense beam contains more photons of the same energy $h\\nu$.\nMore photons eject more electrons, so the number of photoelectrons increases.\nEach photon still carries $h\\nu$, so the kinetic energy $h\\nu - h\\nu_0$ does not change.\nOption (1) is the classical expectation and is wrong. Option (4) fails because there is no time lag at any intensity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $^{14}_{6}\\text{C}$ and $^{14}_{7}\\text{N}$ are isobars.\nReason (R): Isobars contain the same number of neutrons in their nuclei.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: both atoms have mass number 14 but different atomic numbers (6 and 7).\nR is false: isobars share the same mass number, not the same neutron number.\nC-14 has 8 neutrons, while N-14 has 7 neutrons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A path of 1.0 cm in vacuum contains exactly $2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ wavelengths of a radiation X. What is the frequency of X, and to which region of the spectrum does it belong? (Take $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$6.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, visible","$6.0 \\times 10^{12}$ Hz, infrared","$1.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, infrared","$6.0 \\times 10^{16}$ Hz, ultraviolet"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the wavenumber is $\\bar{\\nu} = 2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ cm$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{\\bar{\\nu}} = 5.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ cm $= 5.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ m $= 500$ nm.\nFormula: $\\nu = \\frac{c}{\\lambda} = \\frac{3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{5.0 \\times 10^{-7}}$ Hz.\nSo $\\nu = 6.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nThis lies within $4.0$–$7.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, so X is visible light.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about orbital energies in multi-electron atoms is correct?","image":"","options":["A 2p electron is shielded less than a 2s electron and so experiences a larger effective nuclear charge","For the same n, a 3s electron experiences a larger effective nuclear charge than a 3d electron and is lower in energy","In a multi-electron atom, the energy of an orbital depends only on n","The energy of an orbital of a given type increases as atomic number increases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: s electrons penetrate closer to the nucleus than p electrons, so they are shielded less. The 2s electron therefore feels the larger effective nuclear charge.\n(2) Correct: for the same n, penetration and $Z_{eff}$ follow s > p > d. So 3s is more tightly bound and lower in energy than 3d.\n(3) Incorrect: in multi-electron atoms, energy depends on both n and l.\n(4) Incorrect: as Z increases, $Z_{eff}$ increases, so the orbital energy decreases (becomes more negative).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"The position of an electron (mass $9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg) is determined to within 1.0 nm. What is the minimum uncertainty in its velocity? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s)","image":"","options":["$5.79 \\times 10^{4}$ m s$^{-1}$","$5.79 \\times 10^{5}$ m s$^{-1}$","$7.27 \\times 10^{5}$ m s$^{-1}$","$1.16 \\times 10^{5}$ m s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Delta x = 1.0 \\times 10^{-9}$ m, $m = 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg.\nFormula: $\\Delta x \\cdot m\\Delta v \\geq \\frac{h}{4\\pi}$, so $\\Delta v = \\frac{h}{4\\pi m \\Delta x}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta v = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{4 \\times 3.1416 \\times 9.11 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 10^{-9}} = 5.79 \\times 10^{4}$ m s$^{-1}$.\nOption (3) leaves out $4\\pi$, and option (4) uses $2\\pi$ instead of $4\\pi$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In Rutherford's gold foil experiment, approximately what fraction of the α-particles bounced back, i.e., were deflected by nearly $180^\\circ$?","image":"","options":["About 1 in 20","About 1 in 2,000","About 1 in 20,000","About half of them"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Most α-particles passed through the foil undeflected.\nA small fraction was deflected by small angles.\nOnly a very few, about 1 in 20,000, bounced back by nearly $180^\\circ$.\nThis rare back-scattering showed that the positive charge is concentrated in a very small volume.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) Bohr's model can explain the line spectrum of $\\text{Li}^{2+}$.\n(b) Bohr's model explains the splitting of spectral lines in a magnetic field.\n(c) In a hydrogen atom, the energy of an orbital depends only on the principal quantum number.\n(d) The probability density $|\\psi|^2$ can take negative values in certain regions of an atom.\n(e) The speed of an electron in a Bohr orbit decreases as n increases.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: $\\text{Li}^{2+}$ is a one-electron, hydrogen-like ion.\n(b) Incorrect: Bohr's model fails to explain the Zeeman effect.\n(c) Correct: for H and hydrogen-like species, orbital energy depends only on n.\n(d) Incorrect: $|\\psi|^2$ is always positive.\n(e) Correct: electron speed decreases as the principal quantum number increases.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the maximum number of electrons in an atom that can have the quantum numbers $n = 3$ and $m_l = 0$?","image":"","options":["2","3","6","18"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For $n = 3$, the allowed values of $l$ are 0, 1 and 2.\nEach of these subshells (3s, 3p, 3d) contains exactly one orbital with $m_l = 0$, giving 3 orbitals.\nBy the Pauli exclusion principle, each orbital holds at most 2 electrons with opposite spins.\nSo the maximum is 3 × 2 = 6 electrons.\nOption (4), 18, counts every electron in the $n = 3$ shell and ignores the condition $m_l = 0$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the hydrogen atom, the 2s and 2p orbitals have the same energy.\nReason (R): For hydrogen and hydrogen-like species, the energy of an orbital depends only on the principal quantum number n.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. In one-electron species (H, $He^+$, $Li^{2+}$), orbital energy and size depend only on n.\nA is true. 2s and 2p both have n = 2, so in hydrogen they have the same energy.\nR directly explains A.\nIn multi-electron atoms the energy depends on both n and l, which is why 2s and 2p differ there.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bohr orbits have no real meaning, and their existence can never be demonstrated experimentally.\nReason (R): According to the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, the exact position and exact velocity of an electron cannot be known simultaneously, so a precise path cannot be defined.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A Bohr orbit is a precisely defined circular path, and NCERT states that such orbits have no real meaning.\nR is true. A trajectory requires both the position and the velocity to be known exactly at the same time, and the uncertainty principle forbids this.\nR explains A, because the impossibility of a defined path is exactly why orbits are replaced by orbitals, which describe the electron only in terms of probability.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"The violet end of the visible spectrum is at about 400 nm and the red end at about 750 nm. What is the difference between the wavenumbers of these two radiations, in cm$^{-1}$?","image":"","options":["$1.17 \\times 10^{4}$ cm$^{-1}$","$1.17 \\times 10^{6}$ cm$^{-1}$","$2.86 \\times 10^{4}$ cm$^{-1}$","$3.50 \\times 10^{3}$ cm$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda_1 = 400$ nm $= 4.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ cm and $\\lambda_2 = 750$ nm $= 7.5 \\times 10^{-5}$ cm.\nFormula: $\\bar{\\nu} = \\frac{1}{\\lambda}$.\nSubstituting: $\\bar{\\nu}_1 = 25000$ cm$^{-1}$ and $\\bar{\\nu}_2 = 13333$ cm$^{-1}$.\nSo the difference is $\\approx 1.17 \\times 10^{4}$ cm$^{-1}$.\nOption (3) wrongly takes the reciprocal of the wavelength difference (350 nm), which is not valid.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A light detector registers a signal only when it receives at least $1.0 \\times 10^{-17}$ J of energy. What is the minimum number of photons of wavelength 550 nm that must strike it to produce a signal? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["27","28","36","18"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: required energy = $1.0 \\times 10^{-17}$ J, $\\lambda = 550 \\times 10^{-9}$ m\nFormula: $E_{photon} = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$; number of photons $n = \\frac{E_{total}}{E_{photon}}$\nSubstituting: $E_{photon} = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{550 \\times 10^{-9}} = 3.61 \\times 10^{-19}$ J, so $n = \\frac{1.0 \\times 10^{-17}}{3.61 \\times 10^{-19}} = 27.7$\nEnergy comes only in whole quanta, so 27 photons would fall short. The minimum is the next whole number, 28 photons.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Potassium has a threshold frequency of $5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nStatement I: Red light of frequency about $4.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz will not eject electrons from potassium, however intense the beam is.\nStatement II: When light above the threshold frequency is used, increasing its intensity increases the kinetic energy of each ejected electron.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The frequency is below $\\nu_0$, so each photon has too little energy to free an electron, whatever the intensity.\nStatement II is incorrect. Greater intensity means more photons, so more electrons are ejected. The kinetic energy of each electron, $h(\\nu - \\nu_0)$, depends only on frequency.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Using Bohr's model, calculate the radius of the second stationary state ($n = 2$) of the $He^+$ ion. Take $a_0 = 52.9$ pm.","image":"","options":["105.8 pm","211.6 pm","52.9 pm","26.45 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n = 2$, $Z = 2$ for $He^+$, $a_0 = 52.9$ pm.\nFormula: $r_n = \\frac{52.9\\,n^2}{Z}$ pm\nSubstituting: $r_2 = \\frac{52.9 \\times 4}{2}$\nSo $r_2 = 105.8$ pm.\nThe value 211.6 pm is what you get if you forget to divide by Z, which gives the $n = 2$ radius of hydrogen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a direct consequence of Heisenberg's uncertainty principle?","image":"","options":["Electrons in atoms move in fixed circular orbits of definite radius","The trajectory of an electron in an atom cannot be defined","The energy of an electron in an atom can have any value","Electrons are ejected from a metal only above a threshold frequency"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A trajectory is fixed by knowing both the position and the velocity of a body at each instant.\nHeisenberg's principle says both cannot be known exactly at the same time for an electron. So a definite path or trajectory cannot be defined, which is option (2).\nOption (1) is the Bohr picture, and the principle rules this out.\nOption (4) describes the photoelectric effect, which is a separate phenomenon.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a hydrogen atom, the 3s, 3p and 3d orbitals have the same energy.\nStatement II: For hydrogen and hydrogen-like species, the energy of an orbital depends only on the principal quantum number $n$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct: a one-electron species has no inter-electronic repulsion or shielding, so orbital energy depends only on $n$.\nStatement I is correct: since 3s, 3p and 3d all have $n = 3$, they are degenerate in hydrogen.\nThe splitting into 3s < 3p < 3d happens only in multi-electron atoms, where energy depends on both $n$ and $l$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the wavenumber of a radiation of wavelength 500 nm, expressed in cm$^{-1}$?","image":"","options":["$2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ cm$^{-1}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{6}$ cm$^{-1}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{2}$ cm$^{-1}$","$5.0 \\times 10^{4}$ cm$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 500$ nm $= 500 \\times 10^{-7}$ cm $= 5.0 \\times 10^{-5}$ cm.\nFormula: $\\bar{\\nu} = \\frac{1}{\\lambda}$.\nSubstituting: $\\bar{\\nu} = \\frac{1}{5.0 \\times 10^{-5}\\text{ cm}}$.\nSo $\\bar{\\nu} = 2.0 \\times 10^{4}$ cm$^{-1}$.\nOption (2) is the value in m$^{-1}$, not cm$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Radiation of wavelength 400 nm falls on a clean sodium surface. The work function of sodium is 2.3 eV. What is the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected photoelectrons? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$, 1 eV $= 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ J)","image":"","options":["0.80 eV","1.55 eV","3.10 eV","5.40 eV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 400 \\times 10^{-9}$ m and $W_0 = 2.3$ eV.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{2}m_ev^2 = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda} - W_0$.\nPhoton energy: $E = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{400 \\times 10^{-9}} = 4.97 \\times 10^{-19}$ J. Dividing by $1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ gives $E = 3.10$ eV.\nSubstituting: KE $= 3.10 - 2.3 = 0.80$ eV.\nThe value 3.10 eV is the photon energy itself, not the kinetic energy. The value 5.40 eV wrongly adds the work function instead of subtracting it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An electron (mass $9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg) has a kinetic energy of $1.82 \\times 10^{-24}$ J. Its de Broglie wavelength is closest to (take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s):","image":"","options":["364 nm","182 nm","515 nm","728 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: KE $= 1.82 \\times 10^{-24}$ J, $m = 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg.\nFormula: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,KE}{m}}$ and $\\lambda = \\frac{h}{mv}$.\nSubstituting: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 1.82 \\times 10^{-24}}{9.1 \\times 10^{-31}}} = \\sqrt{4 \\times 10^{6}} = 2000$ m s$^{-1}$.\nThen $\\lambda = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 2000} = 3.64 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nSo $\\lambda$ = 364 nm. The value 515 nm comes from leaving out the factor 2 in KE.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Red light of very high intensity shone on potassium metal for hours does not eject any photoelectrons.\nReason (R): The frequency of red light, $(4.3$ to $4.6) \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, is lower than the threshold frequency of potassium, $5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that red light of any brightness ejects no electrons from potassium, even after hours.\nR is true: red light lies at $(4.3$ to $4.6) \\times 10^{14}$ Hz, while $\\nu_0$ for potassium is $5.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nR explains A: each red photon has energy $h\\nu < h\\nu_0$. Increasing intensity only adds more such photons, so no single photon can eject an electron.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Using the Rydberg constant for hydrogen, $R_H = 109677\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$, calculate the wavenumber of the first (longest-wavelength) line of the Balmer series.","image":"","options":["$15233\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$","$82258\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$","$27419\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$","$5332\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: For the Balmer series $n_1 = 2$. The first line has the longest wavelength, which is the smallest energy gap, so $n_2 = 3$.\nFormula: $\\bar{\\nu} = R_H\\left(\\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \\frac{1}{n_2^2}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $\\bar{\\nu} = 109677\\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}\\right) = 109677 \\times \\frac{5}{36}$\nSo $\\bar{\\nu} \\approx 15233\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$.\nOf the other options, 82258 is the first Lyman line, 27419 is the Balmer series limit and 5332 is the first Paschen line.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The characteristics of cathode rays do not depend on the material of the electrodes or on the nature of the gas in the discharge tube.\nStatement II: The charge to mass ratio of the positively charged particles in canal rays depends on the gas from which they originate.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Cathode rays behave the same whatever the electrode metal or gas. This is why electrons are taken to be basic constituents of all atoms.\nStatement II is correct. Canal-ray particles are positive gaseous ions, so their mass and their e/m depend on the gas in the tube.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about cathode rays produced in a discharge tube is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In the absence of an electric or magnetic field, they travel in straight lines.","They are themselves invisible, but they make a zinc sulphide coating glow when they strike it.","In electric and magnetic fields, they are deflected the way negatively charged particles would be.","The mass of the cathode ray particles changes when the gas filled in the tube is changed."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: with no field present, cathode rays move in straight lines from the cathode towards the anode.\n(2) Correct: the rays cannot be seen directly. They are detected by fluorescent or phosphorescent materials such as ZnS.\n(3) Correct: their deflection in electric and magnetic fields shows that they carry negative charge (electrons).\n(4) Incorrect: the properties of cathode rays do not depend on the electrode material or the gas. A gas-dependent mass is a property of canal rays (positive ions).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following species have exactly five unpaired electrons in their ground state?\nFe$^{3+}$, Mn$^{2+}$, Cr, Co$^{3+}$, Cr$^{+}$, Mn\n(Atomic numbers: Cr = 24, Mn = 25, Fe = 26, Co = 27)","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Fe$^{3+}$: [Ar] $3d^5$, so 5 unpaired.\nMn$^{2+}$: [Ar] $3d^5$, so 5 unpaired.\nCr: [Ar] $3d^5 4s^1$, so 6 unpaired.\nCo$^{3+}$: [Ar] $3d^6$, so 4 unpaired.\nCr$^{+}$: [Ar] $3d^5$ (the 4s electron is lost), so 5 unpaired.\nMn: [Ar] $3d^5 4s^2$, so 5 unpaired.\nFour species (Fe$^{3+}$, Mn$^{2+}$, Cr$^{+}$, Mn) have exactly five unpaired electrons.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ground-state configuration of copper (Z = 29) is [Ar] $3d^{10} 4s^1$ and not [Ar] $3d^9 4s^2$.\nReason (R): Completely filled subshells have extra stability because of symmetrical electron distribution and larger exchange energy.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: copper's actual configuration is [Ar] $3d^{10} 4s^1$.\nR is true: fully filled (and half-filled) subshells are more symmetrical. Their parallel-spin electrons can also exchange positions more often, which releases more exchange energy.\nThis extra stability of the $3d^{10}$ arrangement is exactly why one 4s electron moves into 3d. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An electron (mass $9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg) has a kinetic energy of $2.0 \\times 10^{-24}$ J. What is its de Broglie wavelength? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s.)","image":"","options":["347 nm","491 nm","174 nm","694 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $m = 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg, K.E. $= 2.0 \\times 10^{-24}$ J.\nFormula: K.E. $= \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$ gives $p = mv = \\sqrt{2m\\,K.E.}$, so $\\lambda = \\frac{h}{\\sqrt{2m\\,K.E.}}$.\nSubstituting: $p = \\sqrt{2 \\times 9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-24}} = 1.908 \\times 10^{-27}$ kg m s$^{-1}$.\n$\\lambda = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{1.908 \\times 10^{-27}} = 3.47 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nSo $\\lambda \\approx$ 347 nm. Dropping the factor 2 inside the root gives the wrong value of 491 nm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a black body are correct?\n(i) It is a perfect absorber as well as a perfect radiator of radiant energy.\n(ii) Carbon black approximates fairly closely to a black body.\n(iii) At a given temperature, the intensity of radiation emitted keeps increasing with wavelength without any maximum.\n(iv) In thermal equilibrium it radiates the same amount of energy per unit area as it absorbs from its surroundings in a given time.\n(v) Perfect black bodies are commonly available in practice.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: a black body absorbs and emits all frequencies.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT says carbon black approximates fairly closely to a black body.\n(iii) Incorrect: intensity first rises, reaches a maximum at a particular wavelength, then falls.\n(iv) Correct: a black body in thermal equilibrium emits as much energy as it absorbs.\n(v) Incorrect: no ideal black body exists in practice; a cavity with a tiny hole is only an approximation.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"According to Bohr's model, what is the energy of the electron in the n = 3 stationary state of a hydrogen atom? (Take $R_H = 2.18 \\times 10^{-18}$ J)","image":"","options":["$-7.27 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$-19.62 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$-2.42 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$-0.807 \\times 10^{-19}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: hydrogen, so Z = 1, and n = 3.\nFormula: $E_n = -R_H \\frac{1}{n^2}$\nSubstituting: $E_3 = -\\frac{2.18 \\times 10^{-18}}{9}$ J\nSo $E_3 = -2.42 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nThe value $-7.27 \\times 10^{-19}$ J comes from dividing by n instead of $n^2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An electron moving in a beam has a de Broglie wavelength of 0.50 nm. Its momentum is measured with an uncertainty of 1% of its momentum. Using $\\Delta x \\cdot \\Delta p \\geq \\frac{h}{4\\pi}$, what is the minimum uncertainty in its position?","image":"","options":["3.98 nm","0.398 nm","39.8 nm","7.96 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 0.50$ nm and $\\Delta p = 0.01p$.\nFormula: from de Broglie, $p = \\frac{h}{\\lambda}$, so $\\Delta p = \\frac{0.01h}{\\lambda}$.\nUncertainty principle: $\\Delta x_{min} = \\frac{h}{4\\pi \\Delta p} = \\frac{h\\lambda}{4\\pi (0.01h)} = \\frac{\\lambda}{0.04\\pi}$. The constant h cancels, so the electron's mass is not needed.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta x = \\frac{0.50}{0.1257}$ nm $= 3.98$ nm.\nSo $\\Delta x \\approx$ 3.98 nm. Using $\\frac{h}{2\\pi}$ instead of $\\frac{h}{4\\pi}$ gives the wrong value of 7.96 nm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity/particle) with List-II (experimental method).\nList-I:\n(a) $e/m_e$ of the electron\n(b) Magnitude of charge e\n(c) Neutron\n(d) Canal rays\nList-II:\n(p) Perpendicular electric and magnetic fields applied to a cathode ray beam\n(q) Charged droplets in air ionised by X-rays, moving between charged plates\n(r) Thin beryllium sheet struck by $\\alpha$-particles\n(s) Electrical discharge in a modified cathode ray tube","image":"","options":["(a)-(p), (b)-(q), (c)-(r), (d)-(s)","(a)-(q), (b)-(p), (c)-(r), (d)-(s)","(a)-(p), (b)-(q), (c)-(s), (d)-(r)","(a)-(s), (b)-(q), (c)-(r), (d)-(p)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(p): Thomson found $e/m_e$ by applying electric and magnetic fields perpendicular to each other and to the electron beam.\n(b)-(q): Millikan's oil drops picked up charge from air ionised by X-rays. Their motion between charged plates gave the value of e.\n(c)-(r): Chadwick obtained neutrons by striking beryllium with $\\alpha$-particles.\n(d)-(s): Canal rays were discovered using discharge in a modified cathode ray tube.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The Bohr model cannot correctly describe an electron in an atom as moving in a fixed circular orbit.\nReason (R): A definite path needs both the exact position and the exact velocity of the electron at the same instant, and the Heisenberg uncertainty principle says these cannot be known together.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says the Bohr model treats the electron as a charged particle in well-defined circular orbits. This contradicts the uncertainty principle, and the model also ignores the wave character of the electron.\nR is true. A trajectory is fixed only by knowing position and velocity at every instant. For an electron, these cannot both be found exactly at the same time, so definite paths are ruled out.\nR directly explains why a fixed orbit has no real meaning.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"For a hydrogen-like species, the wavenumber of a spectral line is proportional to $Z^2\\left(\\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \\frac{1}{n_2^2}\\right)$. Which transition in $He^+$ ($Z = 2$) produces a line of the same wavelength as the first line of the Balmer series (n = 3 → n = 2) of hydrogen?","image":"","options":["n = 4 → n = 2","n = 6 → n = 4","n = 3 → n = 2","n = 6 → n = 3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Hydrogen first Balmer line: $1^2\\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}\\right) = \\frac{5}{36}$.\nFor $He^+$ we need $4\\left(\\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \\frac{1}{n_2^2}\\right) = \\frac{5}{36}$, so $\\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \\frac{1}{n_2^2} = \\frac{5}{144} = \\frac{1}{16} - \\frac{1}{36}$.\nThis gives $n_1 = 4$ and $n_2 = 6$, so the transition is 6 → 4.\nThe tempting option 4 → 2 gives $4 \\times \\frac{3}{16} = \\frac{3}{4}$, which matches the first Lyman line of hydrogen instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the frequency of the photon emitted when the electron in a hydrogen atom drops from $n = 4$ to $n = 2$. Use $\\nu = 3.29 \\times 10^{15}\\left(\\frac{1}{n_f^2} - \\frac{1}{n_i^2}\\right)$ Hz.","image":"","options":["$6.17 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$2.47 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz","$4.57 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz","$8.23 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_i = 4$, $n_f = 2$. This is an emission line of the Balmer series.\nFormula: $\\nu = 3.29 \\times 10^{15}\\left(\\frac{1}{2^2} - \\frac{1}{4^2}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $3.29 \\times 10^{15} \\times (0.25 - 0.0625) = 3.29 \\times 10^{15} \\times 0.1875$\nSo $\\nu = 6.17 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nThe value $4.57 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz belongs to the $3 \\to 2$ transition, not $4 \\to 2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In a $\\text{He}^{+}$ ion, an electron falls from n = 4 to n = 2. The emitted photon has the same wavenumber as which spectral line of the hydrogen atom?","image":"","options":["First line of the Lyman series (n = 2 → n = 1)","Second line of the Balmer series (n = 4 → n = 2)","First line of the Paschen series (n = 4 → n = 3)","Series limit of the Balmer series (n = ∞ → n = 2)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For hydrogen-like species, $\\bar{\\nu} = R Z^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{n_f^2} - \\frac{1}{n_i^2}\\right)$.\nFor $\\text{He}^{+}$ (Z = 2), 4 → 2 gives $4\\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{16}\\right)R = \\frac{3}{4}R$.\nFor H (Z = 1), 2 → 1 gives $\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{4}\\right)R = \\frac{3}{4}R$, which matches.\nThe tempting option is H 4 → 2. It gives only $\\frac{3}{16}R$, because it ignores the $Z^2$ factor.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The total number of orbitals with $n = 3$ is 9.\n(ii) For $l = 2$, the magnetic quantum number $m_l$ can take 5 values.\n(iii) The 4f subshell corresponds to $n = 4$, $l = 3$.\n(iv) For $n = 2$, the azimuthal quantum number $l$ can be 2.\n(v) In the hydrogen atom, the energy of an orbital depends on both $n$ and $l$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. The number of orbitals is $n^2 = 9$.\n(ii) Correct. There are $2l+1 = 5$ values (−2 to +2).\n(iii) Correct. f means $l = 3$, which is allowed since $l \\le n-1 = 3$.\n(iv) Incorrect. For $n = 2$, $l$ can only be 0 or 1.\n(v) Incorrect. For H and H-like species, energy depends only on $n$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements regarding Rutherford's experiment and nuclear model are correct?\n(i) The positive charge and most of the mass of the atom are concentrated in the nucleus.\n(ii) Electrons and the nucleus are held together by electrostatic forces of attraction.\n(iii) The gold foil used was very thin, about 100 nm thick.\n(iv) The model explains how electrons are distributed around the nucleus and what their energies are.\n(v) The large deflections of α-particles were caused by attraction from the electrons of gold atoms.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the first postulate of the nuclear model.\n(ii) Correct: electrostatic attraction holds the electrons and the nucleus together.\n(iii) Correct: the foil thickness was about 100 nm.\n(iv) Incorrect: a stated drawback of the model is that it says nothing about electron distribution or electron energies.\n(v) Incorrect: the deflections came from strong repulsion by the concentrated positive charge.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The properties of cathode ray particles are the same whatever the material of the electrodes and whatever gas is present in the discharge tube.\nStatement II: The mass of the positively charged particles in canal rays depends on the gas present in the discharge tube.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Cathode ray characteristics do not depend on the electrode material or the gas, so electrons are a basic constituent of all atoms.\nStatement II is correct. Canal ray particles are positive gaseous ions. Their mass and charge-to-mass ratio depend on the gas they come from.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"All isotopes of a given element show the same chemical behaviour because they have:","image":"","options":["the same mass number","the same number of neutrons","the same number of electrons, fixed by the same number of protons","the same number of nucleons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"According to NCERT, chemical properties are controlled by the number of electrons.\nThe number of electrons in a neutral atom equals the number of protons (Z).\nIsotopes have the same Z, so they have the same number of electrons.\nIsotopes differ in neutron number and therefore in mass number and nucleon count. So options (1), (2) and (4) are wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In cathode ray discharge tube experiments, the characteristics of cathode rays were found to be the same whatever metal was used for the electrodes and whatever gas filled the tube. What did this observation establish?","image":"","options":["Cathode rays are a form of electromagnetic radiation like X-rays","Electrons are a basic constituent of all atoms","The mass of the cathode ray particle depends on the gas used","Cathode rays are positively charged gaseous ions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT: the properties of cathode rays (electrons) do not depend on the electrode material or on the nature of the gas in the tube.\nSo the same particle must come from every kind of atom. This means electrons are a basic constituent of all atoms.\nOption (4) describes canal rays. Their mass does depend on the gas, which is why option (3) is also wrong.\nOption (1) fails because cathode rays are deflected by electric and magnetic fields, unlike X-rays.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the minimum frequency of radiation needed to remove the electron completely from $Li^{2+}$ when the electron is in its first excited state? (Use $\\nu = 3.29 \\times 10^{15} Z^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{n_i^2} - \\frac{1}{n_f^2}\\right)$ Hz)","image":"","options":["$7.40 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz","$3.29 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz","$0.82 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz","$2.47 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Z = 3. The first excited state means $n_i = 2$. Ionisation means $n_f = \\infty$.\nFormula: $\\nu = 3.29 \\times 10^{15} Z^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{n_i^2} - 0\\right)$\nSubstituting: $3.29 \\times 10^{15} \\times 9 \\times \\frac{1}{4}$\nSo $\\nu = 7.40 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz.\nThe value $2.47 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz comes from using Z instead of $Z^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the total number of atomic orbitals in the shell with principal quantum number $n = 4$ (the N shell)?","image":"","options":["16","8","4","32"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The number of orbitals in a shell is $n^2$.\nFor $n = 4$, l = 0, 1, 2, 3. These give 1 (4s) + 3 (4p) + 5 (4d) + 7 (4f) = 16 orbitals, which matches $4^2 = 16$.\n32 is the maximum number of electrons ($2n^2$), not the number of orbitals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"For a system whose energy does not change with time, the Schrödinger equation is written as $\\hat{H}\\psi = E\\psi$. Which of the following correctly describes $\\hat{H}$?","image":"","options":["It is the probability density of the electron at a point","It is the orbital angular momentum of the electron, equal to $\\frac{nh}{2\\pi}$","It is a numerical constant equal to the Rydberg constant","It is a mathematical operator, called the Hamiltonian, constructed from the expression for the total energy of the system"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT calls $\\hat{H}$ the Hamiltonian, a mathematical operator.\nSchrödinger gave a recipe to build it from the total energy of the system. This includes the kinetic energies of all particles, electron–nucleus attraction, and electron–electron and nucleus–nucleus repulsion.\nSolving the equation gives E and $\\psi$.\nProbability density is $|\\psi|^2$, not $\\hat{H}$. The quantity $\\frac{nh}{2\\pi}$ is Bohr's quantised angular momentum.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following series of lines in the hydrogen spectrum:\nLyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett, Pfund\nHow many of these series lie in the infrared region?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Lyman series ($n_1$ = 1) lies in the ultraviolet region.\nBalmer series ($n_1$ = 2) lies in the visible region. It is the only visible series.\nPaschen ($n_1$ = 3), Brackett ($n_1$ = 4) and Pfund ($n_1$ = 5) all lie in the infrared region.\nSo 3 series are in the infrared.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (spectral series of hydrogen) with List-II (lower level $n_1$ and spectral region).\nList-I:\n(A) Lyman\n(B) Balmer\n(C) Brackett\n(D) Pfund\nList-II:\n(p) $n_1 = 2$, visible\n(q) $n_1 = 1$, ultraviolet\n(r) $n_1 = 5$, infrared\n(s) $n_1 = 4$, infrared","image":"","options":["A-q, B-p, C-s, D-r","A-p, B-q, C-r, D-s","A-q, B-p, C-r, D-s","A-q, B-s, C-p, D-r"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lyman: transitions end at $n_1 = 1$ and lie in the ultraviolet, so A-q.\nBalmer: transitions end at $n_1 = 2$ and are the only visible lines, so B-p.\nBrackett: $n_1 = 4$, infrared, so C-s.\nPfund: $n_1 = 5$, infrared, so D-r.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The kinetic energy of photoelectrons ejected from a metal increases when the intensity of the incident light is increased at a fixed frequency.\nReason (R): A more intense beam of light contains a larger number of photons.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false. Kinetic energy of photoelectrons equals $h\\nu - h\\nu_0$, so it depends only on the frequency of light, not on its intensity.\nR is true. A brighter beam has more photons, so more electrons are ejected, but each electron receives the same energy.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"The threshold frequency of a metal is $5.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. Radiation of frequency $1.2 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz falls on it. What is the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electrons? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s)","image":"","options":["$7.951 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$4.307 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$3.644 \\times 10^{-19}$ J","$1.160 \\times 10^{-18}$ J"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 1.2 \\times 10^{15}$ Hz and $\\nu_0 = 5.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{2}m_ev^2 = h(\\nu - \\nu_0)$.\nSubstituting: $6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 6.5 \\times 10^{14}$ J.\nSo KE = $4.307 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nOption (1) is the full photon energy $h\\nu$, and option (3) is the work function $h\\nu_0$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Light of wavelength 400 nm falls on sodium metal, whose work function is 2.3 eV. What is the maximum speed of the ejected photoelectrons? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, $1$ eV $= 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ J, $m_e = 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg)","image":"","options":["$1.04 \\times 10^{6}$ m/s","$3.76 \\times 10^{5}$ m/s","$5.31 \\times 10^{5}$ m/s","$2.82 \\times 10^{5}$ m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Photon energy: $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda} = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^8}{400 \\times 10^{-9}} = 4.97 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nWork function: $W_0 = 2.3 \\times 1.602 \\times 10^{-19} = 3.68 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nKinetic energy: KE $= E - W_0 = 1.28 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nSpeed: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,KE}{m_e}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 1.28 \\times 10^{-19}}{9.1 \\times 10^{-31}}} \\approx 5.31 \\times 10^{5}$ m/s.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following ground-state electronic configurations is INCORRECT?\n(Atomic numbers: Ti = 22, Fe = 26, Co = 27, Cu = 29)","image":"","options":["Ti$^{2+}$ : [Ar] $3d^2$","Fe$^{2+}$ : [Ar] $3d^6$","Co$^{2+}$ : [Ar] $3d^5 4s^2$","Cu$^{+}$ : [Ar] $3d^{10}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When transition metal atoms ionise, electrons are removed from the outermost 4s orbital before 3d.\nTi ([Ar] $3d^2 4s^2$) loses its two 4s electrons to give $3d^2$, which is correct.\nFe ([Ar] $3d^6 4s^2$) gives $3d^6$, which is correct.\nCu ([Ar] $3d^{10} 4s^1$) gives $3d^{10}$, which is correct.\nCo ([Ar] $3d^7 4s^2$) should give Co$^{2+}$ = [Ar] $3d^7$. The listed $3d^5 4s^2$ wrongly removes 3d electrons, so it is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Lamp A is rated 100 W and converts 10% of its power into monochromatic light of wavelength 450 nm. Lamp B is rated 60 W and converts 15% of its power into monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm. What is the ratio of the number of photons emitted per second by A to that by B?","image":"","options":["0.83","1.20","1.48","0.75"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: light power of A = 10% of 100 W = 10 W; light power of B = 15% of 60 W = 9 W\nFormula: $N = \\frac{P}{hc/\\lambda} = \\frac{P\\lambda}{hc}$, so $N \\propto P\\lambda$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{N_A}{N_B} = \\frac{10 \\times 450}{9 \\times 600} = \\frac{4500}{5400}$\nSo the ratio = 0.83. Option (3) results from wrongly taking $N \\propto P/\\lambda$; option (4) ignores the power values.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many protons, neutrons and electrons are present in the ion $^{39}_{19}\\text{K}^{+}$?","image":"","options":["19 protons, 20 neutrons, 19 electrons","19 protons, 20 neutrons, 18 electrons","20 protons, 19 neutrons, 18 electrons","19 protons, 39 neutrons, 20 electrons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Z = 19, so the ion has 19 protons.\nNeutrons = A − Z = 39 − 19 = 20. This holds for both atoms and ions.\nA +1 charge means one electron has been lost, so electrons = 19 − 1 = 18.\nOption (1) counts electrons for the neutral atom and ignores the charge.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) de Broglie relation\n(B) Electron microscope\n(C) Heisenberg uncertainty principle\n(D) Diffraction of an electron beam\nList-II\n(i) $\\Delta x \\cdot \\Delta p_x \\geq \\frac{h}{4\\pi}$\n(ii) Experimental confirmation that electrons behave as waves\n(iii) $p = \\frac{h}{\\lambda}$\n(iv) Magnification of about 15 million times","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): the de Broglie relation $\\lambda = h/p$ can be rearranged to $p = h/\\lambda$.\nB-(iv): the electron microscope uses the wave nature of electrons and magnifies about 15 million times.\nC-(i): Heisenberg's principle is written as $\\Delta x \\cdot \\Delta p_x \\geq h/4\\pi$.\nD-(ii): diffraction of an electron beam confirmed de Broglie's prediction that electrons behave as waves.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An anion $\\text{X}^{3-}$ that has 18 electrons and mass number 31 contains 16 neutrons in its nucleus.\nStatement II: $^{37}_{17}\\text{Cl}^{-}$ and $^{40}_{18}\\text{Ar}$ have the same number of electrons as well as the same number of neutrons.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: a 3− anion has 3 extra electrons, so Z = 18 − 3 = 15. Neutrons = 31 − 15 = 16. Correct.\nStatement II: $\\text{Cl}^{-}$ has 17 + 1 = 18 electrons and 37 − 17 = 20 neutrons.\nAr has 18 electrons and 40 − 18 = 22 neutrons.\nThe electron counts match but the neutron counts do not, so Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In Rutherford's gold foil experiment, about 1 in 20,000 $\\alpha$-particles bounced back, being deflected by nearly $180^\\circ$.\nReason (R): In Thomson's model of the atom, the positive charge is spread uniformly over the whole atom.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. This rebound is exactly what was observed in the scattering experiment.\nR is true. Thomson's model does assume uniformly spread positive charge.\nHowever, a uniform spread of charge would deflect $\\alpha$-particles only slightly, so R cannot explain the rebound. The rebound needs positive charge concentrated in a tiny nucleus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, which of the following best describes an ideal black body?","image":"","options":["A body that reflects all radiation falling on it and emits none","A body that emits and absorbs radiations of all frequencies uniformly","A body that absorbs only visible light and emits only infrared radiation","A body whose emitted radiation depends only on the material it is made of"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT defines a black body as an ideal body that emits and absorbs radiations of all frequencies uniformly.\nIt is both a perfect absorber and a perfect radiator. A cavity with a tiny hole is a good physical approximation.\nOption (1) describes a perfect reflector, the opposite of a black body.\nOption (4) is wrong because black body radiation depends only on temperature, not on the material.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Radiation of wavelength 250 nm falls on a metal whose threshold frequency is $7.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz. Find the de Broglie wavelength of the fastest ejected electron.\nUse $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0 \\times 10^8$ m s$^{-1}$ and $m_e = 9.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg.","image":"","options":["0.551 nm","0.853 nm","1.21 nm","0.603 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 250$ nm, $\\nu_0 = 7.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\nPhoton energy: $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda} = 7.951 \\times 10^{-19}$ J. Work function: $h\\nu_0 = 4.638 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nKinetic energy: KE $= 7.951 \\times 10^{-19} - 4.638 \\times 10^{-19} = 3.313 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nVelocity: $v = \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,\\text{KE}}{m_e}} = 8.53 \\times 10^5$ m s$^{-1}$.\nde Broglie wavelength: $\\lambda = \\frac{h}{m_e v} = 8.53 \\times 10^{-10}$ m = 0.853 nm.\nThe 0.551 nm option wrongly uses the full photon energy as KE.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Using $R_H = 109677\\ cm^{-1}$, find the wavelength of the longest-wavelength line of the Paschen series of hydrogen.","image":"","options":["820 nm","656 nm","1282 nm","1876 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Paschen series, so $n_1 = 3$. The longest wavelength means the smallest energy gap, so $n_2 = 4$.\nFormula: $\\bar{\\nu} = R_H\\left(\\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{1}{16}\\right) = 109677 \\times \\frac{7}{144} \\approx 5331.5\\ cm^{-1}$\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{\\bar{\\nu}} = 1.876 \\times 10^{-4}\\ cm$\nSo $\\lambda \\approx 1876\\ nm$, which lies in the infrared.\nFor comparison, 820 nm is the Paschen series limit, 1282 nm is the 5→3 line, and 656 nm is the first Balmer line.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the quantum mechanical model of the atom are correct?\n(a) $|\\psi|^2$ at a point is the probability density there and is always positive.\n(b) The wave function $\\psi$ by itself has a direct physical meaning.\n(c) An orbital can hold at most two electrons, and they must have opposite spins.\n(d) Bohr orbits can be demonstrated experimentally.\n(e) In multi-electron atoms, orbital energies depend on both n and l.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: $|\\psi|^2$ is the probability density and is always positive.\n(b) Incorrect: $\\psi$ itself has no physical meaning; only $|\\psi|^2$ does.\n(c) Correct: an orbital holds at most two electrons, with $m_s$ = +½ and −½.\n(d) Incorrect: by the uncertainty principle, Bohr orbits have no real meaning and cannot be demonstrated.\n(e) Correct: orbital energy depends on n and l in multi-electron atoms.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Using the Rydberg constant for hydrogen, $R_H = 109677\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$, find the wavelength of the spectral line emitted when the electron in a hydrogen atom falls from $n = 4$ to $n = 2$.","image":"","options":["656 nm","486 nm","434 nm","1875 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $n_1 = 2$, $n_2 = 4$, $R_H = 109677\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\bar{\\nu} = R_H\\left(\\frac{1}{n_1^2} - \\frac{1}{n_2^2}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $\\bar{\\nu} = 109677\\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{16}\\right) = 20564\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$.\nThen $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{\\bar{\\nu}} = 4.863 \\times 10^{-5}$ cm, which is about 486 nm (a visible Balmer line).\nThe 656 nm option is the $3 \\to 2$ line.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following sets of quantum numbers ($n, l, m_l, m_s$) is NOT permissible for an electron in an atom?","image":"","options":["$n = 3, l = 2, m_l = -2, m_s = +\\frac{1}{2}$","$n = 4, l = 0, m_l = 0, m_s = -\\frac{1}{2}$","$n = 3, l = 3, m_l = +1, m_s = +\\frac{1}{2}$","$n = 2, l = 1, m_l = -1, m_s = -\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Rules: l can range from 0 to n − 1; $m_l$ can range from −l to +l; and $m_s$ is ±½.\n(1) This is a 3d electron and every value is allowed.\n(2) This is a 4s electron and every value is allowed.\n(3) For n = 3 the largest allowed l is 2, so l = 3 (a '3f' orbital) does not exist. This set is not permissible.\n(4) This is a 2p electron and every value is allowed.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the energy of one mole of photons of radiation of wavelength 500 nm? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$, $N_A = 6.022 \\times 10^{23}$ mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["239 kJ mol$^{-1}$","120 kJ mol$^{-1}$","399 kJ mol$^{-1}$","479 kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda = 500 \\times 10^{-9}$ m\nFormula: $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$ for one photon; multiply by $N_A$ for one mole.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{500 \\times 10^{-9}} = 3.976 \\times 10^{-19}$ J per photon.\nPer mole: $3.976 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 6.022 \\times 10^{23} = 2.39 \\times 10^{5}$ J mol$^{-1}$\nSo E = 239 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Matching the lines in the emission spectrum of an unknown sample with those of known elements quickly establishes the identity of the sample.\nReason (R): Each element has a unique line emission spectrum.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Characteristic lines in atomic spectra are used in chemical analysis to identify unknown atoms, much as fingerprints identify people.\nR is true. NCERT states that each element has a unique line emission spectrum.\nR is the correct explanation of A, because uniqueness is exactly what makes line-matching a reliable method of identification.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match each observation in List-I, which Bohr's model could NOT explain, with its correct description in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Zeeman effect\n(B) Stark effect\n(C) Fine structure of hydrogen spectrum\n(D) Chemical bonding\nList-II:\n(I) Closely spaced doublet lines seen with sophisticated spectroscopic techniques\n(II) Splitting of spectral lines in a magnetic field\n(III) Ability of atoms to combine and form molecules\n(IV) Splitting of spectral lines in an electric field","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Zeeman effect is the splitting of spectral lines in a magnetic field, so A-II.\n(B) Stark effect is the splitting of spectral lines in an electric field, so B-IV.\n(C) Fine structure refers to the doublets (two closely spaced lines) in the hydrogen spectrum, so C-I.\n(D) Bohr's model could not explain how atoms form molecules through chemical bonds, so D-III.\nOption (2) swaps the magnetic-field and electric-field effects.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The SI unit of wavenumber is m$^{-1}$, although cm$^{-1}$ is the unit more commonly used in spectroscopy.\nStatement II: The wavenumber of a radiation increases as its wavelength increases.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true: wavenumber is the number of wavelengths per unit length. Its SI unit is m$^{-1}$, while cm$^{-1}$ is common but not an SI unit.\nStatement II is false: $\\bar{\\nu} = \\frac{1}{\\lambda}$, so the wavenumber decreases as the wavelength increases.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): According to classical electromagnetic theory, an electron revolving round the nucleus in a circular orbit at constant speed should continuously emit radiation.\nReason (R): A body moving in a circle at constant speed has no acceleration.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. By Maxwell's theory, an accelerated charged particle emits electromagnetic radiation, and an orbiting electron is accelerated.\nR is false. Even at constant speed, the direction of motion keeps changing, so the body is accelerated.\nIt is precisely this acceleration that makes the electron radiate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (scientist) with List-II (contribution).\nList-I:\n(A) Chadwick\n(B) Millikan\n(C) J.J. Thomson\n(D) Röentgen\nList-II:\n(i) Charge on the electron by the oil drop method\n(ii) Neutral particles emitted on bombarding beryllium with α-particles\n(iii) Penetrating rays produced when electrons strike a dense metal target\n(iv) Charge to mass ratio of the electron using crossed electric and magnetic fields","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Chadwick (1932) found neutrons by bombarding beryllium with α-particles, so A-(ii).\n(B) Millikan measured the electronic charge with the oil drop experiment, so B-(i).\n(C) Thomson (1897) measured $e/m_e$ using perpendicular electric and magnetic fields, so C-(iv).\n(D) Röentgen named X-rays, which are produced when electrons strike a dense anode (the target), so D-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: As the temperature of a black body is raised, the maximum of its intensity–wavelength curve shifts towards longer wavelength.\nStatement II: The intensity and spectral distribution of radiation emitted by a black body depend only on its temperature.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. As the temperature rises, the maximum shifts to shorter wavelength. This is why heated iron goes from dull red to white and then to blue.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT states that the amount of light emitted by a black body and its spectral distribution depend only on its temperature.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For a 40 g golf ball moving at 45 m s$^{-1}$, with speed measured to within 2%, the minimum uncertainty in position is of the order of $10^{-33}$ m, which is negligible.\n(ii) For an electron located within about $10^{-8}$ m, the uncertainty in velocity is so large that the picture of electrons moving in fixed Bohr orbits cannot hold.\n(iii) One reason the Bohr model fails is that it does not consider the wave character of the electron.\n(iv) The uncertainty in measuring an electron's position and velocity arises only from imperfect instruments and can be removed with better equipment.\n(v) Light used to locate an electron precisely must have a very short wavelength, and its high-momentum photons change the electron's energy on collision.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\Delta x = \\frac{h}{4\\pi m \\Delta v}$ with $\\Delta v = 0.9$ m s$^{-1}$ gives $\\Delta x \\approx 1.46 \\times 10^{-33}$ m.\n(ii) Correct: $\\Delta v$ comes out of the order of $10^{4}$ m s$^{-1}$, so fixed orbits lose meaning.\n(iii) Correct: the Bohr model ignores the wave nature of the electron.\n(iv) Incorrect: the uncertainty is a fundamental consequence of dual behaviour, not an instrument defect.\n(v) Correct: short-wavelength photons carry a large momentum $p = h/\\lambda$ and disturb the electron.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"NCERT mentions elements that were discovered through spectroscopic analysis. How many of the following elements were discovered in this way?\nRubidium, Caesium, Sodium, Gallium, Helium, Potassium","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists Rb, Cs, Tl, In, Ga and Sc as elements discovered when their minerals were analysed spectroscopically.\nHelium was discovered in the sun by the spectroscopic method.\nFrom the given list, the matches are Rubidium, Caesium, Gallium and Helium, which makes 4.\nSodium and potassium are not in NCERT's list of spectroscopic discoveries.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"For an f subshell ($l = 3$), what are the permitted values of the magnetic quantum number $m_l$?","image":"","options":["0, 1, 2, 3","$-3, -2, -1, 0, +1, +2, +3$","$-2, -1, 0, +1, +2$","$+\\frac{1}{2}, -\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: For a given l, the values of $m_l$ run from $-l$ to $+l$ in steps of one, including zero.\nStep 2: For l = 3 this gives −3, −2, −1, 0, +1, +2, +3, which is 2l + 1 = 7 values, so the f subshell has seven orbitals.\nStep 3: Option (3) is the set of values for a d subshell (l = 2).\nStep 4: Option (4) lists the values of $m_s$, not $m_l$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match the species in List-I with the number of unpaired electrons in their ground state in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Cr (Z = 24)\n(B) $Fe^{3+}$ (Z = 26)\n(C) $Ni^{2+}$ (Z = 28)\n(D) $Cu^{2+}$ (Z = 29)\nList-II\n(I) 1\n(II) 2\n(III) 5\n(IV) 6","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Cr is [Ar] $3d^5 4s^1$. Five unpaired 3d electrons plus one unpaired 4s electron give 6, so A-IV.\n(B) $Fe^{3+}$ is [Ar] $3d^5$. By Hund's rule all five d electrons are unpaired, so B-III.\n(C) $Ni^{2+}$ is [Ar] $3d^8$. Three d orbitals are paired and two are singly occupied, giving 2, so C-II.\n(D) $Cu^{2+}$ is [Ar] $3d^9$. Only one electron is unpaired, so D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A 50 W lamp converts only 20% of the electrical power it consumes into monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm. How many photons does the lamp emit per second? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s and $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["$1.51 \\times 10^{20}$ photons s$^{-1}$","$6.04 \\times 10^{18}$ photons s$^{-1}$","$3.02 \\times 10^{19}$ photons s$^{-1}$","$3.02 \\times 10^{28}$ photons s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: input power = 50 W, efficiency = 20%, λ = 600 nm = $6.0 \\times 10^{-7}$ m.\nLight power emitted = 0.20 × 50 = 10 J s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: energy of one photon, $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{6.0 \\times 10^{-7}} = 3.313 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nPhotons per second = $\\frac{10}{3.313 \\times 10^{-19}} = 3.02 \\times 10^{19}$ s$^{-1}$.\nUsing the full 50 W instead would wrongly give $1.51 \\times 10^{20}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A 60 W lamp emits monochromatic light of wavelength 500 nm. How many photons does it emit per second? (h = $6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, c = $3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$1.51 \\times 10^{20}$","$0.75 \\times 10^{20}$","$3.02 \\times 10^{20}$","$6.04 \\times 10^{20}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: P = 60 J s$^{-1}$, $\\lambda$ = $500 \\times 10^{-9}$ m.\nFormula: $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$, and photons per second = $\\frac{P}{E}$.\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{500 \\times 10^{-9}} = 3.98 \\times 10^{-19}$ J.\nSo photons per second = $\\frac{60}{3.98 \\times 10^{-19}} \\approx 1.51 \\times 10^{20}$ s$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Light shows particle-like behaviour whenever it interacts with matter, as in the photoelectric effect.\nStatement II: Interference and diffraction of light can be explained only by treating light as a stream of photons.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT says radiation displays particle-like properties when it interacts with matter, for example in black-body radiation and the photoelectric effect.\nStatement II is incorrect: interference and diffraction are wave properties of light, shown while it propagates. They are not explained by the photon picture.\nLight therefore has dual behaviour.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (spectral series of hydrogen) with List-II (lower level $n_1$ and spectral region).\nList-I:\n(A) Lyman\n(B) Balmer\n(C) Paschen\n(D) Pfund\nList-II:\n(I) $n_1 = 3$, infrared\n(II) $n_1 = 1$, ultraviolet\n(III) $n_1 = 5$, infrared\n(IV) $n_1 = 2$, visible","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Lyman: $n_1 = 1$, ultraviolet, so it matches II.\n(B) Balmer: $n_1 = 2$, visible, so it matches IV.\n(C) Paschen: $n_1 = 3$, infrared, so it matches I.\n(D) Pfund: $n_1 = 5$, infrared, so it matches III.\nOption (3) is tempting because Paschen and Pfund are both infrared, but it swaps their $n_1$ values.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A 60 W lamp emits monochromatic light of wavelength 600 nm. Assuming all the power is emitted as this light, how many photons does it emit per second? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$1.81 \\times 10^{20}$ s$^{-1}$","$3.62 \\times 10^{20}$ s$^{-1}$","$9.05 \\times 10^{19}$ s$^{-1}$","$1.81 \\times 10^{17}$ s$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: P = 60 J s$^{-1}$, $\\lambda = 600 \\times 10^{-9}$ m\nFormula: number of photons per second $N = \\frac{P}{E}$, where $E = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda}$\nSubstituting: $E = \\frac{6.626 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{600 \\times 10^{-9}} = 3.313 \\times 10^{-19}$ J\n$N = \\frac{60}{3.313 \\times 10^{-19}}$\nSo $N = 1.81 \\times 10^{20}$ photons s$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In Thomson's apparatus for measuring $e/m_e$, the electric and magnetic fields are applied perpendicular to each other and to the electron beam. With only the electric field on, the electrons strike the screen at point A. With only the magnetic field on, they strike point C. With both fields suitably balanced, they strike point B. Which statement correctly describes point B?","image":"","options":["B is the point the beam strikes when neither field is applied, because the two deflections cancel.","B lies beyond A because the combined field deflects the beam the most.","B is where the beam strikes when the magnetic field alone is made stronger.","B is the point struck by positive canal-ray particles in the same tube."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"When only the electric field acts, the beam is deflected to A. When only the magnetic field acts, it is deflected to C.\nThomson balanced the two field strengths so that their deflections cancelled.\nThe electrons then returned to their undeflected path and struck B, the same point they hit with no field applied.\nOption (2) fails because the balanced fields cancel each other rather than add.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: According to de Broglie, every object in motion, including a moving cricket ball, has a wave character.\nStatement II: The wave properties of ordinary moving objects cannot be detected because their large masses make the associated wavelengths extremely short.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that, according to de Broglie, every object in motion has a wave character.\nStatement II is correct. Since $\\lambda = h/mv$, a large mass gives an extremely small wavelength, which is far too short to detect. For example, a 0.1 kg ball moving at 10 m s$^{-1}$ has $\\lambda \\approx 6.6 \\times 10^{-34}$ m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"The plots of the wave function $\\psi$ and the probability density $\\psi^2$ against distance r from the nucleus are drawn for the 1s and 2s orbitals of hydrogen. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["For the 2s orbital, $\\psi^2$ decreases continuously with r and never becomes zero at any finite distance","For the 1s orbital, $\\psi$ changes sign once as r increases","For the 1s orbital, $\\psi^2$ is maximum at the nucleus and falls sharply as r increases","The $\\psi^2$ curves for 1s and 2s have the same form, differing only in scale"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: for 2s, $\\psi^2$ first falls sharply to zero (a radial node) and then rises again before decaying.\n(2) Incorrect: the 1s orbital has no node, so $\\psi$ never changes sign. It is the 2s orbital whose $\\psi$ changes sign once.\n(3) Correct: for 1s the probability density is highest at the nucleus and drops steeply with distance.\n(4) Incorrect: NCERT points out that the 1s and 2s curves are clearly different.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (series in the hydrogen spectrum) with List-II (value of $n_1$, the lower level of the transition).\nList-I:\n(P) Lyman\n(Q) Balmer\n(R) Brackett\n(S) Pfund\nList-II:\n(i) 4\n(ii) 1\n(iii) 5\n(iv) 2","image":"","options":["P-ii, Q-iv, R-i, S-iii","P-ii, Q-iv, R-iii, S-i","P-iv, Q-ii, R-i, S-iii","P-ii, Q-i, R-iv, S-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The series with $n_1$ = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, Brackett and Pfund, in that order.\nSo Lyman → 1 (ii), Balmer → 2 (iv), Brackett → 4 (i) and Pfund → 5 (iii).\nA common slip is to swap Brackett and Pfund.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the maximum number of electrons in an atom that can have the quantum numbers $n = 3$, $m_l = 0$ and $m_s = +\\frac{1}{2}$?","image":"","options":["6","3","9","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For $n = 3$, l can be 0, 1 or 2.\nEach of these subshells has exactly one orbital with $m_l = 0$: 3s, $3p_z$ and $3d_{z^2}$. That makes 3 orbitals.\nEach orbital holds two electrons with opposite spins, so only one electron per orbital can have $m_s = +\\frac{1}{2}$.\nMaximum number = 3 × 1 = 3.\n6 counts both spin states, and 9 counts all orbitals with $n = 3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match each region of the electromagnetic spectrum in List-I with its approximate frequency in List-II.\nList-I\nA. Radio frequency region\nB. Microwave region\nC. Infrared region\nD. Ultraviolet region\nList-II\n(i) about $10^{16}$ Hz\n(ii) about $10^{6}$ Hz\n(iii) about $10^{13}$ Hz\n(iv) about $10^{10}$ Hz","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Radio waves have a frequency of about $10^{6}$ Hz and are used for broadcasting, so A-(ii).\nMicrowaves have a frequency of about $10^{10}$ Hz and are used for radar, so B-(iv).\nInfrared radiation has a frequency of about $10^{13}$ Hz and is used for heating, so C-(iii).\nUltraviolet radiation has a frequency of about $10^{16}$ Hz and is a component of sunlight, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In the ground state of a chromium atom (Z = 24), how many electrons have azimuthal quantum number $l = 0$?","image":"","options":["8","7","6","12"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cr has the exceptional configuration $1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^6 3d^5 4s^1$.\nElectrons with $l = 0$ are those in s orbitals: 1s (2), 2s (2), 3s (2) and 4s (1).\nTotal = 2 + 2 + 2 + 1 = 7.\nThe answer 8 comes from wrongly using the aufbau-expected $4s^2$.\nThe answer 12 counts p electrons ($l = 1$) instead of s electrons.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"According to Table 2.2 of NCERT, which of the following metals has the highest work function?","image":"","options":["Silver","Magnesium","Copper","Lithium"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Work functions listed in Table 2.2: Li 2.42 eV, Na 2.3 eV, K 2.25 eV, Mg 3.7 eV, Cu 4.8 eV, Ag 4.3 eV.\nCopper has the largest value, 4.8 eV.\nSilver (4.3 eV) is the tempting option, but it is lower than copper.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"According to the $(n+l)$ rule, which of the following orbitals has the lowest energy in a multi-electron atom?","image":"","options":["4f","5d","6p","6s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Under the $(n+l)$ rule, the orbital with the lower value of $(n+l)$ has the lower energy.\nThe $(n+l)$ values are: 4f = 4+3 = 7, 5d = 5+2 = 7, 6p = 6+1 = 7, 6s = 6+0 = 6.\nOnly 6s has $(n+l) = 6$, so it has the lowest energy and is filled first.\nThe other three all tie at 7. Among them the lower $n$ is lower in energy (4f < 5d < 6p), but every one of them lies above 6s.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: As the temperature of a black body is increased, the maximum of its intensity–wavelength curve shifts towards shorter wavelength.\nStatement II: An iron rod heated in a furnace first glows dull red, then turns white and finally becomes blue at very high temperature.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT (Fig. 2.8) states that the maxima of the curve shift to shorter wavelength as the temperature rises.\nStatement II is correct. The heated iron rod goes from dull red to more red, then white, then blue. This means a higher proportion of short-wavelength radiation is emitted as temperature rises.\nStatement II is in fact an everyday illustration of Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Bohr's model, the energy of the electron in a hydrogen atom is negative for every allowed orbit.\nStatement II: The zero of energy is assigned to a free electron at rest that is infinitely far from the nucleus ($n = \\infty$).\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $E_n = -R_H/n^2$ is negative for every finite n. The bound electron has lower energy than a free electron.\nStatement II is correct. Setting $n = \\infty$ gives $E_\\infty = 0$. This is the reference state of a free electron at rest.\nAs n decreases, $E_n$ becomes more negative. The ground state (n = 1) is the most stable state.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A neutral atom of tritium contains:","image":"","options":["1 proton, 1 neutron, 1 electron","1 proton, 2 neutrons, 1 electron","2 protons, 1 neutron, 2 electrons","1 proton, 0 neutrons, 1 electron"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Tritium is $^{3}_{1}\\text{T}$, with Z = 1 and A = 3.\nProtons = 1, and electrons = 1 because the atom is neutral.\nNeutrons = 3 − 1 = 2.\nOption (1) describes deuterium and option (4) describes protium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about Bohr's model of the hydrogen atom are correct?\n(i) The energy of an electron in a stationary state does not change with time.\n(ii) The radius of the first Bohr orbit is 52.9 pm.\n(iii) The energy of the n = 2 state is $-0.545 \\times 10^{-18}$ J.\n(iv) For a line in the absorption spectrum, $n_f < n_i$.\n(v) The model explains the splitting of spectral lines in an electric field.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is one of Bohr's postulates about stationary states.\n(ii) Correct: $a_0 = 52.9$ pm.\n(iii) Correct: $E_2 = -\\frac{2.18 \\times 10^{-18}}{4} = -0.545 \\times 10^{-18}$ J.\n(iv) Incorrect: in absorption the electron moves to a higher orbit, so $n_f > n_i$.\n(v) Incorrect: the Stark effect is a known failure of the model.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Bohr orbits have no real meaning, and their existence can never be demonstrated experimentally.\nReason (R): A well-defined path of an electron requires its exact position and exact velocity to be known at the same time, which the Heisenberg uncertainty principle forbids.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that Bohr orbits have no real meaning and cannot be demonstrated experimentally.\nR is true. A trajectory is fixed only by knowing position and velocity simultaneously, which is impossible for an electron.\nR is exactly why A holds: a precise circular path (orbit) cannot exist. An orbital is instead a wave function describing probability.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In an α-scattering set-up, a thin gold foil is surrounded by a circular zinc sulphide screen. If gold atoms had the structure proposed by Thomson, which pattern of flashes would be expected on the screen?","image":"","options":["Flashes spread uniformly all around the screen, including near the source","Flashes mostly straight behind the foil and at small angles, with essentially none on the source side","Flashes mostly on the source side, because most α-particles would be reflected","No flashes at all, because all α-particles would be absorbed by the positive sphere"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In Thomson's model, the mass and positive charge are spread evenly over the atom.\nα-particles have enough energy to pass through such a uniform distribution. They would only slow down and change direction by small angles.\nSo flashes should appear behind the foil and at small angles, with essentially no back-scattering.\nThe actual observation of about 1 in 20,000 bouncing back contradicted this. It led Rutherford to propose a tiny, dense, positive nucleus.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the quantum mechanical model of the atom are correct?\n(i) For a system whose energy does not change with time, the Schrödinger equation is written as $\\hat{H}\\psi = E\\psi$.\n(ii) The Hamiltonian operator is constructed from the total energy of the system, which includes the kinetic energies of the particles, the electron–nucleus attractions and the repulsions among electrons and among nuclei.\n(iii) The Schrödinger equation can be solved exactly for multi-electron atoms such as helium.\n(iv) Orbitals in multi-electron atoms are somewhat contracted compared with those of hydrogen because of the increased nuclear charge.\n(v) In a multi-electron atom, the energy of an orbital depends only on the principal quantum number $n$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This is the form of the Schrödinger equation for a system whose energy does not change with time.\n(ii) Correct. This is Schrödinger's recipe for building $\\hat{H}$ from the total energy.\n(iii) Incorrect. The equation cannot be solved exactly for multi-electron atoms, so approximate methods are used.\n(iv) Correct. The greater nuclear charge contracts all the orbitals somewhat.\n(v) Incorrect. Energy depends on both n and l in multi-electron atoms; it depends on n alone only for hydrogen-like species.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Thomson measured $e/m_e = 1.758820 \\times 10^{11}$ C kg$^{-1}$, and Millikan's oil drop experiment gave $e = 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ C. What mass of the electron do these two results give together?","image":"","options":["$9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg","$1.10 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg","$2.82 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg","$91.1 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $e/m_e = 1.758820 \\times 10^{11}$ C kg$^{-1}$ and $e = 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ C.\nFormula: $m_e = \\frac{e}{(e/m_e)}$.\nSubstituting: $m_e = \\frac{1.602 \\times 10^{-19}}{1.758820 \\times 10^{11}}$.\nSo $m_e \\approx 9.11 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg.\nThis agrees with the accepted value of $9.1094 \\times 10^{-31}$ kg.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"According to the $(n+l)$ rule, which of the following orbitals has the lowest energy in a multi-electron atom?","image":"","options":["4d","5s","5p","4f"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Find $(n+l)$ for each orbital: 4d = 4+2 = 6; 5s = 5+0 = 5; 5p = 5+1 = 6; 4f = 4+3 = 7.\nThe lower the $(n+l)$ value, the lower the energy.\n5s has the smallest value (5), so it is filled first.\nThe tempting option is 4d because it has the smaller n. However, its $(n+l)$ of 6 is higher than that of 5s.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"A species contains 12 protons, 12 neutrons and 10 electrons. Its correct symbol is:","image":"","options":["$^{24}_{12}\\text{Mg}^{2+}$","$^{22}_{10}\\text{Ne}^{2-}$","$^{24}_{12}\\text{Mg}^{2-}$","$^{12}_{12}\\text{Mg}^{2+}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Z = number of protons = 12, so the element is magnesium.\nA = protons + neutrons = 12 + 12 = 24.\nProtons exceed electrons by 2, so the species is a cation with charge +2.\nOption (2) wrongly takes Z from the electron count, and option (4) uses the neutron count as A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In the simplified picture of an electromagnetic wave (NCERT Fig. 2.6), the electric and magnetic field components are compared. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["They have the same wavelength but different frequencies.","They have the same wavelength, frequency, speed and amplitude, and vibrate in mutually perpendicular planes.","The electric field oscillates along the direction of propagation and the magnetic field oscillates perpendicular to it.","Both fields oscillate in the same plane but are out of phase by half a wavelength."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the two components have the same wavelength, frequency, speed and amplitude.\nThey vibrate in two mutually perpendicular planes.\nBoth fields are also perpendicular to the direction of propagation. This rules out option (3).\nOption (1) fails because the frequencies are identical. Option (4) fails because the planes are perpendicular, not the same.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Mass number is the total number of nucleons in the nucleus.\n(ii) In a cation, the number of electrons is greater than the number of protons.\n(iii) Number of neutrons = A − Z holds for neutral atoms as well as ions.\n(iv) Isotopes of an element differ only in the number of neutrons.\n(v) The positive charge of the nucleus is due to both protons and neutrons.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: A = protons + neutrons, which together are the nucleons.\n(ii) Incorrect: a cation has fewer electrons than protons.\n(iii) Correct: ionisation changes only the electron count, not A or Z.\n(iv) Correct: isotopes have the same Z but different numbers of neutrons.\n(v) Incorrect: neutrons are neutral, so the nuclear charge comes from protons only.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the quantum mechanical model of the atom are correct?\n(i) $|\\psi|^2$ at a point is the probability density there and is always positive.\n(ii) The wave function $\\psi$ by itself has a direct physical meaning.\n(iii) An atomic orbital cannot contain more than two electrons.\n(iv) The Schrödinger equation can be solved exactly for multi-electron atoms.\n(v) Quantised electronic energy levels are allowed solutions of the Schrödinger wave equation.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $|\\psi|^2$ is the probability density and is always positive.\n(ii) Incorrect: $\\psi$ itself has no physical meaning; only $|\\psi|^2$ does.\n(iii) Correct: an orbital holds at most two electrons, and they must have opposite spins.\n(iv) Incorrect: for multi-electron atoms only approximate solutions are possible.\n(v) Correct: quantised energy levels arise from the wave-like nature of electrons as allowed solutions.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (hydrogen spectral series) with List-II (lower level $n_1$ and spectral region).\nList-I:\n(A) Lyman\n(B) Balmer\n(C) Paschen\n(D) Pfund\nList-II:\n(i) $n_1$ = 5, infrared\n(ii) $n_1$ = 2, visible\n(iii) $n_1$ = 1, ultraviolet\n(iv) $n_1$ = 3, infrared","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lyman: $n_1$ = 1, ultraviolet, so A-(iii).\nBalmer: $n_1$ = 2, the only visible series, so B-(ii).\nPaschen: $n_1$ = 3, infrared, so C-(iv).\nPfund: $n_1$ = 5, infrared, so D-(i).\nNote that Brackett ($n_1$ = 4) is also infrared but is not listed here.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Thomson model\n(B) Rutherford model\n(C) α-particles\n(D) γ-rays\nList-II\n(I) Neutral high-energy radiation, not made of particles\n(II) Positive charge spread uniformly in a sphere with embedded electrons\n(III) Helium nuclei carrying two units of positive charge\n(IV) Resembles the solar system, with electrons revolving like planets\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-II: Thomson proposed positive charge uniformly distributed, with electrons embedded in it (plum pudding).\nB-IV: Rutherford's nuclear model is like a small solar system, with the nucleus as the sun.\nC-III: α-particles carry +2 charge and 4 u mass. They give helium gas on combining with two electrons, so they are helium nuclei.\nD-I: γ-rays are neutral, high-energy radiation like X-rays and do not consist of particles.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"The ground-state electronic configuration of chromium (Z = 24) is:","image":"","options":["[Ar] $3d^4 4s^2$","[Ar] $3d^5 4s^1$","[Ar] $3d^6$","[Ar] $4s^2 4p^4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Simple aufbau filling would predict [Ar] $3d^4 4s^2$.\nIn reality, one 4s electron shifts into 3d, giving [Ar] $3d^5 4s^1$.\nThis happens because a half-filled 3d subshell gains extra stability from its symmetrical distribution and larger exchange energy.\nSo option (1) is the expected configuration, not the actual one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of atoms are isobars?","image":"","options":["$^{12}_{6}\\text{C}$ and $^{14}_{6}\\text{C}$","$^{35}_{17}\\text{Cl}$ and $^{37}_{17}\\text{Cl}$","$^{14}_{6}\\text{C}$ and $^{14}_{7}\\text{N}$","$^{1}_{1}\\text{H}$ and $^{2}_{1}\\text{H}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Isobars have the same mass number (A) but different atomic numbers (Z).\nC-14 and N-14 both have A = 14, but Z is 6 and 7. So they are isobars.\nThe other pairs have the same Z and different A. They are isotopes, not isobars.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about visible light are correct?\n(i) Its wavelength range extends from about 400 nm to 750 nm.\n(ii) Its frequency range extends from about $4.0 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz to $7.5 \\times 10^{14}$ Hz.\n(iii) Violet light has a higher frequency than red light.\n(iv) Red light has the highest wavenumber in the visible region.\n(v) The visible region is a small portion of the spectrum around $10^{15}$ Hz.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: violet is at about 400 nm and red at about 750 nm.\n(ii) Correct: these frequencies follow from $\\nu = c/\\lambda$ for the two ends.\n(iii) Correct: violet has the shorter wavelength, so it has the higher frequency.\n(iv) Incorrect: red has the longest wavelength, so it has the lowest wavenumber.\n(v) Correct: this matches the NCERT description.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In Rutherford's experiment, most of the α-particles passed through the thin gold foil undeflected.\nReason (R): Most of the space inside an atom is empty.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Most α-particles went straight through the foil, and only about 1 in 20,000 bounced back.\nR is true. Rutherford concluded that the atom is mostly empty space, because the nucleus (about $10^{-15}$ m) is tiny compared with the atom (about $10^{-10}$ m).\nR explains A: α-particles moving through empty space meet nothing that can deflect them.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"For the hydrogen atom, the correct order of energies of the 3s, 3p and 3d orbitals is:","image":"","options":["3s < 3p < 3d","3s > 3p > 3d","3s = 3p = 3d","3s < 3p = 3d"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Hydrogen has only one electron, so there is no electron–electron repulsion and no shielding.\nThe energy of an orbital in hydrogen depends only on n.\nAll orbitals with n = 3 are therefore degenerate: 3s = 3p = 3d.\nThe order 3s < 3p < 3d applies to multi-electron atoms, where energy depends on both n and l.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Use $E_n = -2.18 \\times 10^{-18}\\frac{Z^2}{n^2}$ J and $r_n = \\frac{52.9\\,n^2}{Z}$ pm.\nStatement I: The energy of the electron in the n = 3 orbit of $Li^{2+}$ equals the energy of the electron in the ground state of the hydrogen atom.\nStatement II: The radius of the n = 3 orbit of $Li^{2+}$ equals the radius of the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For $Li^{2+}$, Z = 3 and n = 3.\nEnergy: $E = -2.18 \\times 10^{-18} \\times \\frac{9}{9} = -2.18 \\times 10^{-18}$ J. This equals $E_1$ of hydrogen, so Statement I is correct.\nRadius: $r = \\frac{52.9 \\times 9}{3} = 158.7$ pm. This is not 52.9 pm, so Statement II is incorrect.\nEnergy scales as $\\frac{Z^2}{n^2}$ but radius scales as $\\frac{n^2}{Z}$, so the two need not match together.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many electrons in a ground-state manganese atom (Z = 25) have the magnetic quantum number $m_l = 0$?","image":"","options":["11","12","13","14"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The configuration of Mn is $1s^2 2s^2 2p^6 3s^2 3p^6 3d^5 4s^2$.\nEvery s subshell has $m_l = 0$: the 1s, 2s, 3s and 4s subshells contribute 2 × 4 = 8 electrons.\nThe completely filled 2p and 3p subshells each have one $m_l = 0$ orbital holding 2 electrons, adding 4.\nIn $3d^5$, Hund's rule places one electron in each d orbital, so the $m_l = 0$ orbital holds 1 electron.\nTotal = 8 + 4 + 1 = 13.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In vacuum, radio waves, visible light and X-rays all travel with the same speed.\nReason (R): The speed of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum increases with its frequency.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: in vacuum, all electromagnetic radiations travel at $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$, regardless of wavelength.\nR is false: since $c$ is constant, a higher frequency simply means a shorter wavelength ($\\nu\\lambda = c$). The speed does not change.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the angular momentum of an electron in the third Bohr orbit (n = 3) of a hydrogen atom? (Take $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s)","image":"","options":["$1.05 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s","$3.16 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s","$19.88 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s","$9.49 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: n = 3, $h = 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s.\nFormula (Bohr's quantisation condition): $m_e v r = n\\frac{h}{2\\pi}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{3 \\times 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{2 \\times 3.1416}$\nSo the angular momentum is $3.16 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s.\nThe value $19.88 \\times 10^{-34}$ J s comes from forgetting to divide by $2\\pi$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following could NOT be explained by the electromagnetic (wave) theory of 19th-century classical physics?\n(a) Black-body radiation\n(b) Photoelectric effect\n(c) Variation of heat capacity of solids with temperature\n(d) Line spectra of atoms such as hydrogen\n(e) Diffraction of light\n(f) Interference of light","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a), (b), (c) and (d) cannot be explained by classical theory. Each shows that energy is taken up or given out in discrete amounts.\n(e) Diffraction and (f) interference are explained well by the wave nature of light.\nSo 4 phenomena are unexplained by classical theory.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An electron in $He^+$ falls from the n = 4 orbit to the n = 2 orbit. What is the wavelength of the photon emitted? (Use $\\bar{\\nu} = 1.09677 \\times 10^{7} Z^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{n_f^2} - \\frac{1}{n_i^2}\\right)$ m$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["486.3 nm","243.1 nm","121.6 nm","30.4 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Z = 2, $n_i = 4$, $n_f = 2$.\nFormula: $\\bar{\\nu} = 1.09677 \\times 10^{7} \\times 4 \\times \\left(\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{16}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $\\bar{\\nu} = 1.09677 \\times 10^{7} \\times 4 \\times 0.1875 = 8.226 \\times 10^{6}$ m$^{-1}$\nSo $\\lambda = \\frac{1}{\\bar{\\nu}} = 1.216 \\times 10^{-7}$ m $= 121.6$ nm.\nThis is the same as the hydrogen Lyman line (2 → 1). The value 486.3 nm comes from ignoring $Z^2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"What is the maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in a subshell with azimuthal quantum number $l = 3$?","image":"","options":["7","6","14","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A subshell has $(2l+1)$ orbitals, so for $l = 3$ there are $2(3)+1 = 7$ orbitals (the f subshell).\nBy the Pauli exclusion principle, each orbital holds at most 2 electrons, and they must have opposite spins.\nMaximum electrons = $7 \\times 2 = 14$.\nThe value 7 counts only the orbitals, not the electrons.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about black body radiation is correct?","image":"","options":["At a given temperature, the intensity of radiation emitted by a black body keeps increasing as wavelength increases.","As the temperature of a black body is raised, the maximum of its intensity–wavelength curve shifts towards shorter wavelength.","The spectral distribution of radiation from a black body depends on the material it is made of.","A black body is a perfect absorber but a poor emitter of radiant energy."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: at a fixed temperature, intensity rises with wavelength, reaches a maximum, and then falls.\n(2) Correct: as temperature increases, the maximum of the curve moves to shorter wavelength. This is why a heated iron rod glows red, then white, then blue.\n(3) Incorrect: the intensity and spectral distribution of black body radiation depend only on temperature.\n(4) Incorrect: a black body is also a perfect radiator. It is in thermal equilibrium and emits as much energy as it absorbs.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Chadwick (1932) discovered the neutron. Which target did he bombard, and with which particles?","image":"","options":["A thin gold foil bombarded with $\\alpha$-particles","A thin sheet of beryllium bombarded with $\\alpha$-particles","Hydrogen gas bombarded with cathode rays","A thin sheet of beryllium bombarded with protons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Chadwick bombarded a thin sheet of beryllium with $\\alpha$-particles.\nThis released electrically neutral particles whose mass was slightly greater than that of the proton. He named them neutrons.\nThe gold foil with $\\alpha$-particles in option (1) is Rutherford's scattering experiment, which led to the nucleus, not the neutron.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: According to Table 2.1, the mass of a neutron (1.00867 u) is slightly greater than the mass of a proton (1.00727 u).\nStatement II: The electron has a relative charge of $-1$, and its approximate mass in u is taken as 0.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The neutron's mass, 1.00867 u, is a little more than the proton's, 1.00727 u. This matches Chadwick's observation of particles slightly heavier than protons.\nStatement II is correct. The electron's relative charge is $-1$. Its mass is only 0.00054 u, so its approximate mass is listed as 0.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An FM radio station broadcasts at a frequency of 100 MHz. What is the wavelength of the radiation emitted by its transmitter? (Take $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["3.0 m","0.30 m","30 m","300 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 100$ MHz $= 1.0 \\times 10^{8}$ Hz and $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{1.0 \\times 10^{8}}$ m.\nSo $\\lambda = 3.0$ m, which lies in the radio-wave region.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Heavy water contains deuterium in place of protium. Calculate the total number of neutrons present in 2.0 g of $\\text{D}_2{}^{16}\\text{O}$. (Atomic masses: D = 2 u, O = 16 u; $N_A = 6.022 \\times 10^{23}\\ \\text{mol}^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$6.02 \\times 10^{23}$","$6.69 \\times 10^{23}$","$4.82 \\times 10^{23}$","$1.20 \\times 10^{24}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: molar mass of $\\text{D}_2\\text{O}$ = 2(2) + 16 = 20 g/mol, so 2.0 g = 0.1 mol.\nNeutrons per molecule = 2 × (2 − 1) + (16 − 8) = 2 + 8 = 10.\nTotal neutrons = 0.1 × 10 × $6.022 \\times 10^{23}$.\nSo the number of neutrons = $6.02 \\times 10^{23}$.\nOption (2) wrongly uses a molar mass of 18 g/mol, and option (3) counts only the 8 neutrons of oxygen.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Light of wavelength 300 nm falls on a metal whose threshold frequency is $5.0\\times10^{14}$ Hz. What is the de Broglie wavelength of the fastest ejected electron? Take $h = 6.626\\times10^{-34}$ J s, $c = 3.0\\times10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$ and $m_e = 9.1\\times10^{-31}$ kg.","image":"","options":["0.853 nm","0.603 nm","1.21 nm","300 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = c/\\lambda = 3.0\\times10^8/(300\\times10^{-9}) = 1.0\\times10^{15}$ Hz.\nFormula: $KE = h(\\nu-\\nu_0)$ and $\\lambda = h/\\sqrt{2m_eKE}$.\nSubstituting: $KE = 6.626\\times10^{-34}\\times5.0\\times10^{14} = 3.313\\times10^{-19}$ J.\nThen $\\sqrt{2\\times9.1\\times10^{-31}\\times3.313\\times10^{-19}} = 7.77\\times10^{-25}$ kg m s$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\lambda = 6.626\\times10^{-34}/7.77\\times10^{-25} = 8.53\\times10^{-10}$ m = 0.853 nm.\nOption (2) wrongly uses $h\\nu$ instead of the KE.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following could NOT be explained by 19th-century classical physics, according to NCERT?\n(i) Black-body radiation\n(ii) Photoelectric effect\n(iii) Diffraction of light\n(iv) Interference of light\n(v) Variation of heat capacity of solids with temperature\n(vi) Line spectrum of hydrogen","image":"","options":["2","3","4","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Not explained: Planck needed quantisation to explain black-body radiation.\n(ii) Not explained: Einstein explained the photoelectric effect using photons.\n(iii) and (iv) Explained: diffraction and interference follow from the wave nature of light.\n(v) Not explained: listed by NCERT among the failures of classical physics.\n(vi) Not explained: line spectra point to discrete energy levels.\nSo the count is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which series of lines in the emission spectrum of atomic hydrogen is the only one that appears in the visible region of the electromagnetic spectrum?","image":"","options":["Lyman series","Paschen series","Balmer series","Pfund series"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The Balmer series ($n_1 = 2$) is the only hydrogen series whose lines fall in the visible region.\nThe Lyman series ($n_1 = 1$) lies in the ultraviolet.\nThe Paschen, Brackett and Pfund series ($n_1 = 3, 4, 5$) all lie in the infrared.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of atoms are isobars?","image":"","options":["$^{12}_{6}$C and $^{13}_{6}$C","$^{35}_{17}$Cl and $^{37}_{17}$Cl","$^{14}_{6}$C and $^{14}_{7}$N","$^{1}_{1}$H and $^{2}_{1}$H"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Isobars have the same mass number A but different atomic number Z.\n$^{14}_{6}$C and $^{14}_{7}$N both have A = 14 but different Z (6 and 7), so they are isobars.\nThe other three pairs have the same Z but different A. They differ only in the number of neutrons, so they are isotopes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Photons of energy 2.35 eV fall separately on clean surfaces of Li, Na, K, Mg, Cu and Ag. Their work functions (in eV) are Li 2.42, Na 2.3, K 2.25, Mg 3.7, Cu 4.8 and Ag 4.3. On how many of these metals will photoelectric emission occur?","image":"","options":["1","3","4","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Emission occurs only when the photon energy is greater than the work function $W_0$.\nNa: 2.3 < 2.35, so it emits. K: 2.25 < 2.35, so it emits.\nLi: 2.42 > 2.35, so it does not emit, although it is the closest miss.\nMg, Cu and Ag have work functions far above 2.35 eV, so none of them emit.\nTotal = 2.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In $He^+$, the electron occupies a Bohr orbit whose energy equals the ground-state energy of a hydrogen atom. What is the radius of this orbit? (Take $a_0 = 52.9$ pm)","image":"","options":["52.9 pm","105.8 pm","26.45 pm","211.6 pm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Z = 2 for $He^+$. The hydrogen ground-state energy is $-2.18 \\times 10^{-18}$ J (Z = 1, n = 1).\nEquating energies: $\\frac{Z^2}{n^2} = 1$, so $\\frac{4}{n^2} = 1$, which gives n = 2.\nFormula: $r_n = \\frac{52.9\\, n^2}{Z}$ pm\nSubstituting: $r = \\frac{52.9 \\times 4}{2}$\nSo r = 105.8 pm.\nThe value 211.6 pm comes from ignoring Z.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"An orbital has principal quantum number $n = 5$ and azimuthal quantum number $l = 1$. Which notation correctly designates it?","image":"","options":["5s","5p","5d","4p"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: The number in the notation is n, which is 5 here.\nStep 2: The letter comes from l: l = 0 is s, l = 1 is p, l = 2 is d and l = 3 is f.\nStep 3: With l = 1 the letter is p, so the orbital is 5p.\nStep 4: 4p has the correct letter but the wrong value of n.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Ultraviolet radiation from the sun has a frequency of about $1.0 \\times 10^{16}$ Hz. What is its wavelength? (Take $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["30 nm","3.0 nm","300 nm","0.30 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\nu = 1.0 \\times 10^{16}$ Hz and $c = 3.0 \\times 10^{8}$ m s$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\lambda = \\frac{c}{\\nu}$.\nSubstituting: $\\lambda = \\frac{3.0 \\times 10^{8}}{1.0 \\times 10^{16}}$ m $= 3.0 \\times 10^{-8}$ m.\nConverting with 1 nm $= 10^{-9}$ m: $\\lambda = 30$ nm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantum number) with List-II (information it provides).\nList-I:\n(A) $n$\n(B) $l$\n(C) $m_l$\n(D) $m_s$\nList-II:\n(i) Orientation of the electron's spin\n(ii) Spatial orientation of the orbital with respect to standard axes\n(iii) Three-dimensional shape of the orbital\n(iv) Shell, size of the orbital and, to a large extent, its energy","image":"","options":["A-iii, B-iv, C-i, D-ii","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) n identifies the shell and fixes the orbital's size and largely its energy, so A matches (iv).\n(B) l defines the shape of the orbital, so B matches (iii).\n(C) $m_l$ gives the orbital's orientation in space, so C matches (ii).\n(D) $m_s = \\pm\\frac{1}{2}$ gives the orientation of the electron's spin, so D matches (i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-CHEM-ATOM-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Structure of Atom","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Structure of Atom","type":"mcq","question":"In a Millikan-type oil drop experiment, which of the following could NOT be the measured charge on an oil droplet? (Take $e = 1.602 \\times 10^{-19}$ C)","image":"","options":["$3.204 \\times 10^{-19}$ C","$2.403 \\times 10^{-19}$ C","$4.806 \\times 10^{-19}$ C","$6.408 \\times 10^{-19}$ C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Millikan concluded that the charge on any droplet is $q = ne$, where n = 1, 2, 3, ...\nCheck each option by dividing by e:\n(1) 3.204/1.602 = 2, allowed.\n(2) 2.403/1.602 = 1.5, not an integer, so this charge is impossible.\n(3) 4.806/1.602 = 3, allowed.\n(4) 6.408/1.602 = 4, allowed.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Structure of Atom","difficulty":"easy"}]});
